LZ.data.lpapers.b1.push({id:"b1l1",title:"Listening Test 1",topics:"Everyday situations · A summer art course · Opinions · A young baker",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You will hear a woman talking to her son.",q:"Where are the car keys?",o:["in her bag","on the kitchen table","in his coat pocket"],a:2,x:"‘Sorry, Mum – I think they're still in my coat pocket.’ Her bag and the kitchen table are the places she has already looked.",script:[["F","Tom, have you seen the car keys? I need to go to the supermarket."],["M","Aren't they in your bag? That's where you usually keep them."],["F","I've looked – they're not there. I was sure I'd left them on the kitchen table, but there's nothing there except the newspaper."],["M","Oh, wait. I borrowed the car yesterday to go to football. Sorry, Mum – I think they're still in my coat pocket. I'll get them."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a man talking about his holiday.",q:"What did he enjoy most?",o:["the museums","the beach","the food"],a:2,x:"‘What I'll really remember is the food.’ It was too windy to swim, and the museums were ‘nothing special’.",script:[["M","The hotel was right next to the beach, but to be honest it was too windy to swim most days. We visited a couple of museums when it rained, which were fine, but nothing special. What I'll really remember is the food. Every evening we tried a different little restaurant in the old town, and the fish was amazing. I've even bought a cookbook so I can make some of the dishes at home."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a girl leaving a message for a friend.",q:"What time will they meet?",o:["at 6.30","at 7.30","at 8.00"],a:1,x:"‘Shall we meet at half past seven instead?’ Half past six was the old plan and eight is when the film starts.",script:[["F","Hi Jess, it's Mia. I've just checked the cinema website, and the film doesn't start at seven like we thought – it starts at eight. So we don't need to meet at half past six any more. Shall we meet at half past seven instead? That gives us time to buy some popcorn. Call me back if that's a problem. Bye!"]]},{ctx:"You will hear a man asking about something he lost at a sports centre.",q:"What has the man lost?",o:["a pair of glasses","a watch","a phone"],a:1,x:"‘It's a watch – a black one with a leather strap.’ He has his phone, and the glasses belong to someone else.",script:[["M","Excuse me, I was here for a swim this morning, and I think I left something in the changing room."],["F","What is it? We've had a few phones handed in today."],["M","No, I've got my phone here. It's a watch – a black one with a leather strap. It was a present from my wife."],["F","Let me look. There's a pair of glasses and some keys… ah, yes. Is this it?"],["M","That's it! Thank you so much."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a woman talking about how she gets to work.",q:"How does she usually travel to work now?",o:["by bike","by bus","by car"],a:0,x:"‘Now I cycle in almost every day.’ She used to drive and she takes the bus only ‘a few times a month’.",script:[["F","When I started this job, I used to drive, but parking in the city centre is so expensive. Then I tried the bus for a while, but it was always late and really crowded. So last spring I bought a bike, and now I cycle in almost every day. When the weather's really bad I still take the bus, but that's only a few times a month. I feel much healthier."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a teacher talking to a class about a school trip.",q:"What must the students bring?",o:["a packed lunch","some money","a waterproof jacket"],a:2,x:"‘You absolutely must bring a waterproof jacket.’ The tickets are paid for and lunch is provided.",script:[["F","OK, everyone, just a reminder about tomorrow's trip to the castle. You don't need to bring any money – the school has already paid for the tickets. And lunch will be provided in the castle café, so no sandwiches this time. But the forecast says it's going to rain in the afternoon, and we'll be outside a lot, so you absolutely must bring a waterproof jacket. Anyone without one won't be able to come on the walk round the walls."]]},{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a birthday present.",q:"What will they buy for their friend?",o:["a book","a concert ticket","a plant"],a:2,x:"‘Let's get her something green for the living room.’ – ‘A plant! Good idea.’ She has too many books and the tickets are too expensive.",script:[["M","What shall we get Sophie for her birthday? She loves reading – how about a book?"],["F","She's got hundreds of books. And she said she hasn't got space on her shelves."],["M","What about a ticket for that concert next month?"],["F","They're too expensive – and I'm not sure she likes that band any more. She's just moved into her new flat, and she said it needs a bit of colour. Let's get her something green for the living room."],["M","A plant! Good idea. There's a nice shop near the station."]]}]},2:{extracts:[{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a film they have seen.",q:"What does the girl say about the film?",o:["The story was hard to follow.","It was longer than it needed to be.","The actors were disappointing."],a:1,x:"‘Did it need to be three hours? The middle part… could have been half as long.’ The story was ‘easy enough to follow’ and the actors were ‘brilliant’.",script:[["M","So what did you think? I loved it."],["F","Yeah, it was good. The story was easy enough to follow, and the actors were brilliant – especially the girl who played the sister. But honestly, did it need to be three hours? The middle part, with all the travelling, could have been half as long."],["M","Maybe. I didn't notice the time."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a boy telling a friend about a climbing club.",q:"How does the boy feel about the club?",o:["disappointed with the coach","worried that it is too difficult for him","surprised that the members are so helpful"],a:2,x:"He expected people wouldn't want to help a beginner, ‘but it's not like that at all’ – people he'd never met showed him what to do.",script:[["F","How's the climbing club going? You've been three times now, haven't you?"],["M","Yeah. I was really nervous before the first session, because I thought everyone would be much better than me and wouldn't want to help a beginner. But it's not like that at all. People I'd never met before showed me what to do and cheered when I got to the top. The coach is good too, but it's the other members who make it."],["F","That's great."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a woman telling a friend about a cookery class.",q:"Why did she join the class?",o:["to meet new people","to cook better meals for her family","to learn to cook food from another country"],a:2,x:"She ‘fell in love with the food’ in Thailand and signed up for a Thai cookery class. She's ‘not really there to make friends’.",script:[["M","I didn't know you were doing a cookery class."],["F","It's my second month. My family's always said my cooking's fine, and I'm not really there to make friends, although people are nice. It's that I went to Thailand last year and fell in love with the food. So when I saw a Thai cookery class advertised at the college, I signed up immediately. Last week we made a green curry – it was nearly as good as the ones I had in Bangkok!"]]},{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a new shopping centre.",q:"What do they agree about?",o:["It is difficult to get to.","The shops are too expensive.","It is too crowded at weekends."],a:2,x:"She says ‘there were people everywhere’ on Saturday; he ‘could hardly move’ on Sunday and won't go at the weekend again – ‘Me neither.’ They disagree about the prices.",script:[["M","Have you been to the new shopping centre yet?"],["F","Last Saturday. It took ages to find a parking space – there were people everywhere."],["M","Tell me about it. I went on Sunday and I could hardly move. I won't go at the weekend again."],["F","Me neither. The prices are OK, though – not more than in town."],["M","I thought some of the shops were quite expensive, actually. But it's easy to get there – the bus stops right outside."],["F","That's true."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a man talking to a friend about his new job.",q:"What does he like best about it?",o:["the people he works with","the hours he works","the money he earns"],a:1,x:"‘The best thing is I finish at three on Fridays, and I start at ten every day.’ The pay isn't much better and he doesn't know his colleagues yet.",script:[["F","How's the new job?"],["M","Really good. The pay isn't much better than at my old company, and I don't really know my colleagues yet – they seem friendly enough. But the best thing is I finish at three on Fridays, and I start at ten every day, so I don't have to get up at six any more. I've got so much more time for myself."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a girl talking to her brother about a concert.",q:"What is she going to do?",o:["watch the concert online","try to get a ticket for another night","go to the concert with her brother"],a:1,x:"An extra concert has been added on Sunday and she's ‘going to set my alarm and try again’. Watching online ‘is not the same’.",script:[["M","Did you get a ticket for Saturday's concert?"],["F","No, they sold out in about five minutes. I'm so annoyed."],["M","They're showing it live online, you know. You could watch it with me."],["F","It's not the same as being there. Anyway, they've just added an extra concert on Sunday. The tickets go on sale tomorrow at nine, so I'm going to set my alarm and try again."],["M","Good luck!"]]}]},3:{title:"Summer Art Course",ctx:"You will hear a woman called Karen Hill talking on the radio about a summer art course for teenagers.",script:[["F",`Hello, and thanks for having me on the programme. I'm Karen Hill, and I run the summer art course for young people aged thirteen to seventeen. This year the course is moving. For the last five years we've been at the college, but this summer we'll be in the old library in the town centre, which has been beautifully repaired and has lots of light – perfect for painting.

The course lasts three weeks, starting on the fourteenth of July, and we meet every weekday morning from ten until one. In the first week, students learn to draw people. It's something many young artists find difficult, so we start with it. In the second week we move on to painting, and in the last week everyone makes something from recycled materials, like old bottles and newspapers.

At the end of the course there's an exhibition of everyone's work in the town hall, and families are welcome to come along. The course costs ninety-five pounds, which includes all materials, but students need to bring their own apron, because it can get quite messy!

To book a place, please don't phone the college. You need to fill in a form on our website, which is young artists dot org. Places are limited to twenty-five students, so book soon.`]],items:[{s:"Place: the {g} in the town centre",a:["old library","library"],x:"‘this summer we'll be in the old library in the town centre’ – the college is where it used to be."},{s:"The course starts on: {g}",a:["14 July","14th July","14th of July","fourteenth of July","the fourteenth of July","July 14","July 14th","the 14th of July","14/7","14.7"],x:"‘starting on the fourteenth of July’"},{s:"Week 1: students learn to draw {g}",a:["people"],x:"‘In the first week, students learn to draw people.’"},{s:"Week 3: students make things from {g} materials",a:["recycled"],x:"‘everyone makes something from recycled materials, like old bottles and newspapers’"},{s:"Students must bring their own {g}",a:["apron","an apron","aprons"],x:"‘students need to bring their own apron’ – materials are included in the price."},{s:"To book, fill in a form on the {g}",a:["website","web site","internet"],x:"‘please don't phone the college. You need to fill in a form on our website’"}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a young woman called Emma Green, who has opened her own bakery.",script:[["M","My guest today is Emma Green, who opened her own bakery last year at the age of just twenty-two. Emma, welcome."],["F","Thanks for inviting me."],["M","When did you first get interested in baking?"],["F","People always expect me to say it was my grandmother who taught me, but actually nobody in my family bakes. When I was about ten, I watched a baking competition on TV and I just thought, I want to try that. My first cake was a disaster, but I kept practising every weekend."],["M","Did you study baking after school?"],["F","I did a course at college for two years. I learnt a lot there, especially about bread, which is much harder than cakes. But the most useful thing was working in a hotel kitchen at the weekends. That's where I learnt to work fast and keep calm when things go wrong."],["M","Why did you decide to open your own bakery?"],["F","I'd always dreamed of it, but I thought I'd wait until I was thirty and had saved some money. Then the little shop on my street became empty, and the rent was surprisingly low. My parents helped me a bit, and I got a small loan from the bank. I thought, if I don't do it now, somebody else will take the shop."],["M","What's a normal day like?"],["F","I get up at four, which my friends think is crazy. The hardest thing isn't the early start, though – I'm used to that now. It's that I'm on my feet for ten hours a day. By the evening my legs really hurt! But I love the moment when I open the door at seven and the first customers come in."],["M","What's your most popular product?"],["F","I thought it would be my chocolate cake, but it's actually a simple cheese and onion roll. The builders working on the new school next door buy about thirty every morning! Cakes sell well at the weekends, when families come in."],["M","And what's next for you?"],["F","Some people have asked me to open a second shop, but I don't want to get too big. What I'd really like is to start baking classes for children on Saturday afternoons, when the shop is quieter. I'd like to give other kids the chance I had."],["M","Emma Green, thank you very much."]],qs:[{q:"What first made Emma interested in baking?",o:["a member of her family","a television programme","a lesson at school"],a:1,x:"‘I watched a baking competition on TV and I just thought, I want to try that.’ ‘Nobody in my family bakes.’"},{q:"What does Emma say was most useful for her?",o:["her college course","her weekend job","advice from a famous baker"],a:1,x:"‘The most useful thing was working in a hotel kitchen at the weekends.’"},{q:"Why did Emma open her bakery earlier than she planned?",o:["A shop in a good place became available.","Her parents offered to pay for everything.","She had saved more money than she expected."],a:0,x:"‘The little shop on my street became empty, and the rent was surprisingly low… if I don't do it now, somebody else will take the shop.’ Her parents only ‘helped a bit’."},{q:"What does Emma find hardest about her job?",o:["getting up early","standing up all day","dealing with customers"],a:1,x:"‘The hardest thing isn't the early start… It's that I'm on my feet for ten hours a day.’"},{q:"What is the most popular product in Emma's bakery?",o:["chocolate cake","a kind of bread roll","biscuits"],a:1,x:"‘I thought it would be my chocolate cake, but it's actually a simple cheese and onion roll.’"},{q:"What does Emma want to do in the future?",o:["open another shop","write a book about baking","teach children to bake"],a:2,x:"‘What I'd really like is to start baking classes for children on Saturday afternoons.’ She doesn't want a second shop."}]}}}),LZ.data.lpapers.b1.push({id:"b1l2",title:"Listening Test 2",topics:"Everyday situations · A wildlife park · Opinions · A cycling trip",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You will hear a man phoning a hotel.",q:"Which room does he want?",o:["a single room","a double room","a family room"],a:1,x:"‘Just a double, please.’ His wife is coming too, so not a single, and the children are staying with their grandparents.",script:[["M","Hello, I'd like to book a room for next Friday and Saturday. It was going to be just me, so I was going to ask for a single, but my wife's decided to come too. Our children are staying with their grandparents, so we won't need a family room. Just a double, please – with a view of the sea, if possible."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a girl talking about her weekend.",q:"What did she do on Saturday?",o:["She went swimming.","She played tennis.","She went shopping."],a:0,x:"‘In the end I went to the swimming pool with my brother.’ Tennis was on Sunday, and they didn't go shopping.",script:[["F","I was supposed to play tennis with Anna on Saturday morning, but it rained all day, so we couldn't. We thought about going shopping instead, but Anna had to help her dad. So in the end I went to the swimming pool with my brother. It was really busy because of the weather, but it was fun. On Sunday it was sunny, so Anna and I finally played tennis."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a woman talking to a shop assistant.",q:"How much will she pay for the T-shirt?",o:["£15","£12","£20"],a:1,x:"‘I can give it to you for twelve pounds’ because it has a small mark. £20 is the normal price and £15 is for the ones by the window.",script:[["F","Excuse me, how much is this T-shirt? There's no price on it."],["M","Those ones are twenty pounds."],["F","Oh. The sign in the window said everything was fifteen pounds."],["M","That's only for the ones on the table by the window. But this one has a small mark on it here, look – so I can give it to you for twelve pounds."],["F","Great, I'll take it."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a boy talking about his new hobby.",q:"What is his new hobby?",o:["taking photos","playing the guitar","painting"],a:0,x:"‘What I've really got into is taking pictures.’ He hasn't touched the guitar much and isn't good at art.",script:[["M","My uncle's a musician, and he gave me his old guitar for my birthday, but to be honest I haven't touched it much. What I've really got into is taking pictures. My mum lent me her old camera, and now I go out every weekend to photograph birds in the park. I'd like to paint them one day, but I'm not very good at art."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a woman leaving a message for a friend.",q:"Where will they meet?",o:["outside the station","in a café","at the museum"],a:1,x:"‘Why don't we meet at the café opposite the museum instead?’ The station is too noisy, and they'll walk to the museum later.",script:[["F","Hi Sarah, it's Kate. About tomorrow – I know we said we'd meet outside the station, but they're doing work on the road there and it's really noisy. Why don't we meet at the café opposite the museum instead? It's called Lemon Tree. We can have a coffee and then walk over to the museum together. See you at ten!"]]},{ctx:"You will hear a weather forecast on the radio.",q:"What will the weather be like tomorrow afternoon?",o:["rainy","sunny","windy"],a:1,x:"‘The afternoon will be bright and sunny.’ The rain is in the morning and the wind ‘will finally drop’.",script:[["M","And now the weather for tomorrow. It'll be a cloudy start in most places, with some heavy rain in the morning, especially in the north. That rain will clear by lunchtime, though, and the afternoon will be bright and sunny, with temperatures reaching twenty-two degrees. The wind that we've had all week will finally drop, so it'll feel quite warm."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a girl talking to her father about a school project.",q:"What does she need help with?",o:["finding information","using the computer","drawing a map"],a:2,x:"‘It's the map of the river that I'm stuck on… You're good at drawing.’ She already has the information and has typed it.",script:[["F","Dad, can you help me with my geography project?"],["M","Sure. Do you need help finding information? I could take you to the library."],["F","No, I've got loads of information from the internet already, and I've typed most of it. It's the map of the river that I'm stuck on. I've tried three times and it looks terrible. You're good at drawing."],["M","OK, let's have a look."]]}]},2:{extracts:[{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a party.",q:"How did the boy feel at the party?",o:["too tired to enjoy it","happy to see old friends","bored because he didn't know anyone"],a:2,x:"He'd have been fine ‘if I'd had someone to talk to’ – everyone was Lucy's cousins and neighbours. He says tiredness ‘wasn't it’.",script:[["F","Did you enjoy Lucy's party on Saturday?"],["M","Honestly, not really. I'd been working all day, but it wasn't that – I'd have been fine if I'd had someone to talk to. I thought some people from our class would be there, but they were all Lucy's cousins and neighbours. I just stood in the kitchen eating crisps."],["F","Oh no!"]]},{ctx:"You will hear a woman telling a friend about a new restaurant.",q:"What does she think about it?",o:["The service was slow.","The food was better than she expected.","It was too expensive."],a:1,x:"She ‘didn't expect much’, but ‘the pizza was one of the best I've ever had’. The waiters were quick and it wasn't expensive.",script:[["M","You went to that new Italian place, didn't you?"],["F","Yes, on Friday. I didn't expect much, because the reviews online weren't great. But the pizza was one of the best I've ever had! The waiters were quick and friendly, and it wasn't expensive at all. I'm definitely going back."]]},{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a TV series.",q:"What does the boy want to do?",o:["watch the next episode with his friend","stop watching the series completely","read the book the series is based on"],a:2,x:"‘I think I'll borrow it from her’ – the book, which his sister says is better. He'd ‘rather not’ watch the next episode together.",script:[["F","Have you seen the latest episode of The Island?"],["M","Yes, but I'm not sure I'll carry on with it. Every episode is the same – someone gets lost, then someone finds them."],["F","It gets better, I promise. Why don't we watch the next one together on Friday?"],["M","Hmm, I'd rather not. Actually, my sister says the book it's based on is much better. I think I'll borrow it from her."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a man telling a friend about his new flat.",q:"What does he like most about the flat?",o:["the size of the rooms","the view from the window","the quiet neighbours"],a:1,x:"‘I can see the whole city and the mountains behind it. I never get tired of that.’ The rooms are small and the neighbours play music late.",script:[["F","How's the new flat?"],["M","Great. The rooms aren't very big, to be honest – my sofa only just fits. And the neighbours upstairs play music quite late sometimes. But when I open the curtains in the morning, I can see the whole city and the mountains behind it. I never get tired of that."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a girl telling a friend about learning Spanish.",q:"Why is she learning Spanish?",o:["She needs it for her job.","She is going to live in Spain.","She wants to talk to her boyfriend's family."],a:2,x:"His parents and grandparents ‘don't speak much English’ and she wants ‘to be able to chat to them properly’. She doesn't need it for work.",script:[["M","I didn't know you were learning Spanish."],["F","I started in January. Everyone thinks it's for work, but I don't need it for my job at all. My boyfriend's from Mexico, and his parents and grandparents don't speak much English. We're visiting them in the summer, and I really want to be able to chat to them properly."]]},{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a football match.",q:"What do they agree about?",o:["The team played badly.","The tickets were too expensive.","The weather was terrible."],a:1,x:"She won't pay forty pounds again – ‘You're right. It's far too much.’ They disagree about the weather, and the team played brilliantly.",script:[["M","What a match! Three–nil!"],["F","I know, the team played brilliantly. The weather was awful, though – I was soaked."],["M","Really? I didn't mind the rain. It was worse last week."],["F","Well, anyway, I'm not paying forty pounds for a ticket again. That's crazy."],["M","You're right. It's far too much for a local match."]]}]},3:{title:"Green Valley Wildlife Park",ctx:"You will hear a man called David giving information to visitors at a wildlife park.",script:[["M",`Good morning, everyone, and welcome to Green Valley Wildlife Park. My name's David, and I'm going to tell you about your visit today. The park opened in nineteen ninety-eight, and it now has more than two hundred kinds of animals. You've arrived at a great time, because we've just got two new baby elephants, which were born in March. You'll find them near the lake.

If you want to see the animals being fed, the best time is half past two, when our keepers feed the lions. Please don't feed any of the animals yourselves – some of them can get very ill from human food.

There are three places to eat in the park. The largest is the restaurant by the main gate, but if you want something quick, the café next to the monkey house sells sandwiches and cold drinks. Please stay on the paths at all times.

We have a small train that goes round the whole park. It leaves every twenty minutes from the station beside the gift shop, and it's free for everyone today. Finally, the park closes at six o'clock, but the last entry to the reptile house is at five. Have a lovely day!`]],items:[{s:"The park now has more than {g} kinds of animals.",a:["200","two hundred"],x:"‘it now has more than two hundred kinds of animals’ – 1998 is when it opened."},{s:"The baby elephants can be seen near the {g}.",a:["lake","the lake"],x:"‘You'll find them near the lake.’"},{s:"The keepers feed the lions at {g}.",a:["2.30","2:30","half past two","two thirty","14.30","14:30","2.30 pm","2:30 pm"],x:"‘the best time is half past two, when our keepers feed the lions’"},{s:"For something quick to eat, go to the café next to the {g}.",a:["monkey house"],x:"‘the café next to the monkey house sells sandwiches and cold drinks’"},{s:"The train leaves from beside the {g}.",a:["gift shop"],x:"‘It leaves every twenty minutes from the station beside the gift shop.’"},{s:"The last entry to the reptile house is at {g}.",a:["5","five","five o'clock","5 o'clock","5.00","5:00","5 pm","5pm","17.00","17:00"],x:"‘the last entry to the reptile house is at five’ – six is when the park closes."}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a teenager called Jake Morris, who cycled across Europe with his father.",script:[["F","Today I'm talking to Jake Morris, who spent last summer cycling two thousand kilometres from London to Rome with his dad. Jake, what gave you the idea?"],["M","It was my dad's idea, really. He'd read a book about a man who cycled around the world, and he said, why don't we do something like that? I thought he was joking at first. I'd never cycled further than my school."],["F","How did you prepare?"],["M","We started training in January. Every Saturday we rode a bit further. I thought my legs would be the problem, but actually the hardest part was learning to repair the bikes. Dad made me practise changing tyres in the garden until I could do it in five minutes."],["F","Where did you sleep?"],["M","We took a small tent, and we camped most nights. Sometimes we stayed in cheap hotels when it rained. The best night was in Switzerland, when a farmer let us sleep in his barn and his family gave us dinner."],["F","Were there any bad moments?"],["M","In France, Dad fell off his bike and hurt his arm. We had to stop for four days. I was worried we'd have to go home, but the doctor said it wasn't broken. Actually, those four days were really nice – we explored a little town and I tried snails for the first time!"],["F","What did you learn from the trip?"],["M","I used to spend hours on my phone, and I got bored really easily. On the trip there often wasn't any signal, and I found I didn't miss it. I learnt that I can be happy with very little. And I got to know my dad much better."],["F","What's next?"],["M","Dad wants to cycle to Norway next summer, but I've got exams, so I'll have to say no. Maybe after that. For now, I want to write about the trip for my school website, so other students can see it's possible."],["F","Jake, thanks for talking to us."]],qs:[{q:"Whose idea was the trip?",o:["Jake's","Jake's father's","a writer's"],a:1,x:"‘It was my dad's idea, really.’ The writer only inspired his dad."},{q:"What did Jake find most difficult when he was preparing?",o:["cycling long distances","learning to repair the bikes","finding time to train"],a:1,x:"‘I thought my legs would be the problem, but actually the hardest part was learning to repair the bikes.’"},{q:"Where did Jake spend his best night of the trip?",o:["in a small hotel","in a tent by a lake","on a farm"],a:2,x:"‘The best night was in Switzerland, when a farmer let us sleep in his barn.’"},{q:"How did Jake feel when his father hurt his arm?",o:["angry about losing time","worried that the trip would end","sure that his father would be fine"],a:1,x:"‘I was worried we'd have to go home.’"},{q:"What does Jake say he learned on the trip?",o:["He doesn't need his phone to be happy.","He wants to be a professional cyclist.","He should have trained harder."],a:0,x:"‘There often wasn't any signal, and I found I didn't miss it. I learnt that I can be happy with very little.’"},{q:"What is Jake planning to do next?",o:["cycle to Norway","write about the trip","take part in a race"],a:1,x:"‘For now, I want to write about the trip for my school website.’ He has to say no to Norway because of exams."}]}}}),LZ.data.lpapers.b1.push({id:"b1l3",title:"Listening Test 3",topics:"Everyday situations · A summer festival · Opinions · A young film-maker",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You will hear a boy talking to his mother.",q:"What does he want for his birthday?",o:["a bike","a phone","a guitar"],a:2,x:"‘What I'd really love is a guitar.’ His bike is fine now, and his phone still works.",script:[["M","Mum, about my birthday – I know I said I wanted a new bike, but mine's actually fine now that Dad's fixed it."],["F","What about a phone? Yours is really old."],["M","It still works. What I'd really love is a guitar. Jake's teaching me, and I have to borrow his every time."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a woman talking about her new flat.",q:"Which room does she like best?",o:["the kitchen","the living room","the bedroom"],a:1,x:"‘The living room is lovely… I spend most of my time in there.’ The kitchen is tiny and the bedroom is noisy.",script:[["F","The kitchen's tiny – I can hardly turn round in it. The bedroom's OK, but it faces the road, so it's a bit noisy. But the living room is lovely. It gets the sun all afternoon, and there's a big window looking over the park. I spend most of my time in there."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a man phoning a cinema.",q:"How many tickets does he buy?",o:["two","three","four"],a:1,x:"‘There were going to be four of us, but my sister can't come now, so just three.’",script:[["M","Hello, I'd like tickets for the half past seven film tonight, please. There were going to be four of us, but my sister can't come now, so just three. Two adults and one child."],["F","Three tickets – that's twenty-four pounds, please."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a girl talking about her weekend.",q:"What did she do on Sunday?",o:["She went to a museum.","She played tennis.","She visited her grandmother."],a:0,x:"Her friend had free tickets for the science museum, ‘so we went there instead’. Tennis and Grandma were on Saturday.",script:[["F","On Saturday I played tennis with my cousin in the morning, and then in the afternoon we visited Grandma. I was going to stay at home on Sunday, but my friend had free tickets for the science museum, so we went there instead. It was great."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a teacher talking to her students.",q:"What time will the bus leave?",o:["at 8.00","at 8.15","at 8.30"],a:1,x:"‘The bus will leave at a quarter past.’ Students must be at school by eight.",script:[["F","Right, everyone, tomorrow's trip. Please be at school by eight o'clock. We'll check that everyone's here, and the bus will leave at a quarter past. We should arrive at about half past nine."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a man talking about his lost bag.",q:"Where did he leave his bag?",o:["on the train","in a café","at the gym"],a:1,x:"‘I went back to the café, and it was still there.’ He only thought he'd left it on the train.",script:[["M","I thought I'd left it on the train, so I called the station, but nobody had handed it in. Then I remembered I'd stopped for a coffee after the gym. I went back to the café, and it was still there, under the table!"]]},{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about what to eat.",q:"What will they eat?",o:["pizza","curry","salad"],a:2,x:"‘Let's just make a big salad, then.’ – ‘Good idea.’",script:[["M","Shall we get a pizza?"],["F","We had pizza yesterday. How about a curry?"],["M","It's so hot today, though. I don't really want anything heavy."],["F","True. Let's just make a big salad, then. There are lots of vegetables in the fridge."],["M","Good idea."]]}]},2:{extracts:[{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a concert.",q:"What did the boy think of the concert?",o:["The band played too loudly.","It was shorter than he expected.","The tickets were good value."],a:2,x:"‘For twenty pounds, I thought it was really worth the money.’ It lasted three hours, and loud is ‘normal’.",script:[["F","How was the concert?"],["M","Brilliant! It went on for nearly three hours, and they played all my favourite songs. It was loud, of course, but that's normal. And for twenty pounds, I thought it was really worth the money."],["F","Lucky you!"]]},{ctx:"You will hear a girl telling a friend about a new teacher.",q:"How does she feel about the teacher?",o:["She finds her lessons fun.","She thinks she's too strict.","She's disappointed with her."],a:0,x:"‘Her lessons are actually fun – she uses games and puzzles.’",script:[["M","What's the new maths teacher like?"],["F","I was worried, because everyone said she was really strict. And she does give lots of homework. But her lessons are actually fun – she uses games and puzzles, and the time goes really quickly."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a man talking to a friend about his holiday.",q:"What was the problem with the holiday?",o:["the weather","the hotel","the food"],a:1,x:"‘The hotel was awful – the room was dirty.’ The weather was perfect and the restaurants were great.",script:[["F","Did you have a good holiday?"],["M","The beach was beautiful, and the weather was perfect every day. The restaurants were great too. But the hotel was awful – the room was dirty and the air conditioning didn't work. We complained, but they didn't do anything."]]},{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a TV programme.",q:"What do they agree about?",o:["It's too long.","The presenter is funny.","It's better than last year."],a:1,x:"He says ‘the presenter is really funny’ and she agrees: ‘She makes me laugh every week.’ They disagree about last year.",script:[["M","Did you watch the cooking programme last night?"],["F","Yes! I think it's better than last year's series."],["M","Hmm, I'm not sure. It's a bit long, too. But the presenter is really funny."],["F","Oh, she's brilliant. She makes me laugh every week."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a woman telling a friend about learning to swim.",q:"Why did she decide to learn?",o:["Her doctor told her to.","She's going on a beach holiday.","Her children wanted her to."],a:2,x:"‘My kids… kept asking me to come in the water with them.’ Her doctor's advice ‘isn't why’.",script:[["M","I didn't know you couldn't swim."],["F","I never learnt as a child. My doctor's always telling me I should do more exercise, but that's not why. My kids go swimming every week, and they kept asking me to come in the water with them. So I thought, why not?"]]},{ctx:"You will hear a boy talking to his sister about a lost key.",q:"What is he going to do?",o:["look for the key again","phone their dad again","wait at a neighbour's house"],a:2,x:"‘Mrs Brown next door said I can stay at her house… I'll go there now.’",script:[["F","Did you find your key?"],["M","No, I've looked everywhere. I tried calling Dad, but he's in a meeting until six."],["F","So what are you going to do?"],["M","Mrs Brown next door said I can stay at her house until someone gets home. I'll go there now."]]}]},3:{title:"Riverside Summer Festival",ctx:"You will hear a man talking on the radio about a summer festival in his town.",script:[["M",`Good morning! I'm Paul Grant from the Riverside Festival team, and I'm here to tell you about this year's festival, which takes place on the last weekend in July. It's our twentieth festival, so we're planning something special. As always, it's held in Victoria Park, next to the river.

The festival starts on Saturday at ten o'clock in the morning and finishes on Sunday evening with fireworks at half past nine. This year there'll be more than thirty bands playing on three stages, and for the first time we've got a special tent for comedy. There's plenty for children too – face painting, a magic show and a boat race on the river on Sunday afternoon.

Entry to the festival is free, but you'll need to buy a ticket if you want to use the car park, which costs five pounds a day. We'd really prefer people to come by bike or on foot if they can.

We're still looking for volunteers to help with things like picking up litter and giving out information. If you'd like to help, please email the festival office. Volunteers get a free T-shirt and lunch on both days!`]],items:[{s:"The festival takes place on the last weekend in {g}.",a:["July"],x:"‘the last weekend in July’"},{s:"This year is the festival's {g} year.",a:["twentieth","20th"],x:"‘It's our twentieth festival.’"},{s:"The fireworks on Sunday start at {g}.",a:["9.30","9:30","half past nine","nine thirty","21.30","21:30","9.30 pm","9:30 pm"],x:"‘fireworks at half past nine’ – ten o'clock is when it starts on Saturday."},{s:"New this year: a special tent for {g}.",a:["comedy"],x:"‘for the first time we've got a special tent for comedy’"},{s:"The car park costs {g} a day.",a:["£5","5","five pounds","5 pounds","£5.00"],x:"‘which costs five pounds a day’ – entry is free."},{s:"Volunteers get a free T-shirt and {g}.",a:["lunch","free lunch","a free lunch"],x:"‘Volunteers get a free T-shirt and lunch on both days.’"}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a teenager called Sophie Lane, who has won a prize for a short film.",script:[["M","Today I'm talking to sixteen-year-old Sophie Lane, whose short film has just won a national prize. Sophie, congratulations!"],["F","Thanks!"],["M","How did you get into film-making?"],["F","My uncle works in television, and when I was twelve he gave me an old video camera. At first I just filmed my cat and my little brother. Then I saw a competition for young film-makers online and decided to make a proper film."],["M","What's your prize-winning film about?"],["F","It's about an old man who teaches a teenager to play chess. I got the idea from my grandad, who taught me to play. I wanted to show that young and old people can learn a lot from each other."],["M","Was it difficult to make?"],["F","The filming was fine – we did it in four weekends. The hardest part was the editing. I'd never done it before, and I had to watch lots of online videos to learn how. I spent hours in my bedroom cutting and changing things."],["M","Who were the actors?"],["F","My grandad played the old man! He was really nervous at first, but he loved it in the end. And my friend Leo played the teenager. Neither of them had acted before."],["M","What did you win?"],["F","A week at a film studio in London, where I'll work with professional directors. I'm really excited. And my film will be shown at the cinema in my town, which is amazing."],["M","What's next for you?"],["F","I'd love to study film at university, but my parents want me to do something more practical as well. We've agreed I'll study film and business. That way I'll learn how to find money for my films!"]],qs:[{q:"How did Sophie start making films?",o:["She did a course at school.","Her uncle gave her a camera.","She won a competition."],a:1,x:"‘My uncle… gave me an old video camera.’ The competition came later."},{q:"Where did the idea for her film come from?",o:["a book she had read","her own grandfather","a news story"],a:1,x:"‘I got the idea from my grandad, who taught me to play.’"},{q:"What did Sophie find most difficult?",o:["finding actors","editing the film","filming at weekends"],a:1,x:"‘The hardest part was the editing.’ The filming ‘was fine’."},{q:"How did her grandfather feel about acting?",o:["He didn't want to do it.","He enjoyed it after a nervous start.","He found it boring."],a:1,x:"‘He was really nervous at first, but he loved it in the end.’"},{q:"What was Sophie's prize?",o:["some new film equipment","a week at a film studio","money for her next film"],a:1,x:"‘A week at a film studio in London.’"},{q:"What does Sophie plan to study?",o:["film only","film and business","acting"],a:1,x:"‘We've agreed I'll study film and business.’"}]}}}),LZ.data.lpapers.b1.push({id:"b1l4",title:"Listening Test 4",topics:"Everyday situations · A cookery course · Opinions · A volunteer in Peru",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You will hear a woman leaving a message for a friend.",q:"Where will they meet?",o:["at the bus stop","outside the station","in the car park"],a:1,x:"‘Let's meet outside the station instead.’ The car park is closed and the bus stop is too busy.",script:[["F","Hi, Kate, it's Lucy. About tomorrow – I know we said the car park, but it's closed for repairs all week. I thought about the bus stop, but it's always so crowded in the morning. Let's meet outside the station instead. See you at nine!"]]},{ctx:"You will hear a boy talking about his favourite sport.",q:"Which sport does he enjoy most?",o:["football","basketball","swimming"],a:1,x:"‘Basketball's the one I really love.’ He plays football because his friends do and swims for his health.",script:[["M","I play football on Saturdays because all my friends are in the team, and I go swimming twice a week because my doctor says it's good for my back. But basketball's the one I really love. I'd play it every day if I could."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a man talking to a shop assistant.",q:"What is wrong with the jacket?",o:["It's the wrong colour.","It's too small.","A button is missing."],a:1,x:"‘It's a bit tight across the shoulders… Have you got a larger size?’ He likes the colour and the buttons are fine.",script:[["M","I bought this jacket last week. I love the colour, and I checked all the buttons before I left the shop. But when I got home I realised it's a bit tight across the shoulders. Have you got it in a larger size?"],["F","Let me have a look for you."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a girl talking to her father.",q:"How will she get to her friend's house?",o:["by car","on foot","by bike"],a:2,x:"‘I'll just take my bike.’ Her dad can't drive her, and it's too far to walk.",script:[["F","Dad, can you drive me to Emma's?"],["M","Sorry, love, the car's at the garage today."],["F","Oh. It's too far to walk, really. Well, it's not raining, so I'll just take my bike."],["M","Good idea. Don't forget your helmet."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a woman talking about a hotel.",q:"What did she like about the hotel?",o:["the food","the swimming pool","the staff"],a:2,x:"‘What made the stay special was the people who worked there.’ The food was ordinary and the pool was closed.",script:[["F","The food was nothing special, and the pool was closed while we were there, which was a shame. But what made the stay special was the people who worked there. They were so friendly and helpful – nothing was too much trouble."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a man phoning a dentist.",q:"When is his appointment?",o:["Tuesday","Wednesday","Thursday"],a:2,x:"‘Thursday at two is fine.’ Tuesday was full and he works on Wednesday.",script:[["F","We haven't got anything on Tuesday, I'm afraid. How about Wednesday morning?"],["M","I work on Wednesdays. Is there anything later in the week?"],["F","Thursday at two o'clock?"],["M","Thursday at two is fine. Thanks."]]},{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a present.",q:"What will they buy for Sam?",o:["a video game","a T-shirt","a book"],a:2,x:"‘Let's get him that book about football, then.’ He has too many T-shirts, and games are too expensive.",script:[["F","What shall we get Sam? A T-shirt?"],["M","He's got loads of T-shirts. What about a video game?"],["F","They're really expensive, though."],["M","True. He's crazy about football – let's get him that book about football, then."],["F","Perfect."]]}]},2:{extracts:[{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a new café.",q:"What does the girl think of the café?",o:["It's too expensive.","The cakes are delicious.","It's too crowded."],a:1,x:"‘The cakes are amazing – the best I've ever had.’ She says the prices are ‘OK’.",script:[["M","Have you tried the new café on the corner?"],["F","Yes, I went on Saturday. It was quite busy, but we found a table. The prices are OK. And the cakes are amazing – the best I've ever had."],["M","I'll have to go, then!"]]},{ctx:"You will hear a boy telling a friend about a school trip.",q:"How did he feel during the trip?",o:["bored","tired","excited"],a:1,x:"‘I was so tired… I'd only slept about four hours.’ The trip itself was interesting.",script:[["F","How was the trip to the castle?"],["M","It was actually really interesting, but I was so tired I could hardly keep my eyes open. I'd only slept about four hours because I was worried about missing the bus."]]},{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a film.",q:"What does the man say about the film?",o:["The ending was surprising.","The actors were disappointing.","It was too long."],a:0,x:"‘I didn't see that ending coming at all.’ He says the actors were great and the time went quickly.",script:[["F","So, what did you think of the film?"],["M","Brilliant! The actors were great, and the two hours went really quickly. And I didn't see that ending coming at all – I was sure the brother was the thief!"]]},{ctx:"You will hear a woman talking to a friend about her new job.",q:"What does she like most about her new job?",o:["the salary","the people","the hours"],a:2,x:"‘The best thing is that I finish at three, so I can pick up the kids.’ The salary is the same, and she doesn't know her colleagues yet.",script:[["M","How's the new job going?"],["F","Really well. The money's about the same as before, and I haven't really got to know the people yet. But the best thing is that I finish at three, so I can pick up the kids from school."]]},{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about the weekend.",q:"What do they decide to do?",o:["go to the beach","go to the cinema","go for a walk"],a:2,x:"‘Let's walk up the hill and have a picnic.’ – ‘OK.’ The beach will be too crowded and the cinema is ‘a waste of a nice day’.",script:[["M","It's going to be sunny on Saturday. Shall we go to the beach?"],["F","Everyone else will be there too. It'll be packed."],["M","The cinema, then?"],["F","It seems a waste of a nice day to sit inside. Let's walk up the hill and have a picnic."],["M","OK, that sounds good."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a girl telling her mother about an exam.",q:"How does she feel about the exam?",o:["relieved","disappointed","nervous"],a:0,x:"‘I'm just glad it's finished – I was really worried about it.’",script:[["F","Mum, the exam's over!"],["M","How did it go?"],["F","Not bad, I think. The last question was hard, but I answered everything. I'm just glad it's finished – I was really worried about it all week."]]}]},3:{title:"Cookery course for teenagers",ctx:"You will hear a woman talking about a cookery course for teenagers.",script:[["F",`Hello, everyone. My name's Anna Webb, and I run the Young Cooks course here at the community centre. It's for teenagers aged between thirteen and seventeen, and it starts on the fifth of October.

The course lasts eight weeks, and classes are on Wednesday evenings from six to eight. You don't need any experience – we start with simple things like soups and salads, and by the end you'll be able to cook a three-course meal for your family.

In the last week, there's a competition, and the winner gets a cookery book signed by a famous chef, Tom Harris, who used to be a student here himself.

The course costs forty pounds, and that includes all the ingredients. You just need to bring an apron and a container to take your food home in.

We only have twelve places, so book soon! You can book online or at the reception desk, which is open every day except Sunday.`]],items:[{s:"The course starts on {g} October.",a:["5th","5","fifth","the fifth","5th of"],x:"‘it starts on the fifth of October’"},{s:"Classes are on {g} evenings.",a:["Wednesday","Wednesdays"],x:"‘classes are on Wednesday evenings from six to eight’"},{s:"By the end, students can cook a three-course {g}.",a:["meal","dinner"],x:"‘you'll be able to cook a three-course meal for your family’"},{s:"The competition prize is a signed {g}.",a:["cookery book","cookbook","book","recipe book"],x:"‘the winner gets a cookery book signed by a famous chef’"},{s:"The course costs £{g}, including ingredients.",a:["40","forty","£40"],x:"‘The course costs forty pounds.’"},{s:"Students should bring an apron and a {g}.",a:["container","food container","box"],x:"‘bring an apron and a container to take your food home in’"}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a young man called Jack Dale, who spent six months as a volunteer in Peru.",script:[["F","My guest today is Jack Dale, who's just come back from six months in Peru. Jack, why did you decide to go?"],["M","I'd finished school and I wasn't sure what to study at university. My cousin had volunteered in South America the year before, and she said it changed her life. So I thought it might help me decide."],["F","What did you do there?"],["M","I worked in a small village school in the mountains, helping the teachers with English lessons. I also helped build a new classroom – I'd never done anything like that before!"],["F","What was the most difficult thing?"],["M","Everyone expects me to say the food or the altitude, but I got used to those quite quickly. It was the language. I'd done a bit of Spanish at school, but the people in the village spoke very fast, and some spoke Quechua, a local language. For the first month I understood almost nothing."],["F","Where did you live?"],["M","With a family – a mother, a father and four children. They didn't have much money, but they were so generous. They treated me like a son. We still talk on video every Sunday."],["F","What's the best memory you have?"],["M","On my last day, the children at the school sang a song they'd written for me. I'm not usually emotional, but I couldn't stop crying!"],["F","And did the trip help you decide what to study?"],["M","Yes, definitely. I realised how much I enjoyed working with children, so I'm going to train to be a primary school teacher. I start in September."]],qs:[{q:"Why did Jack decide to go to Peru?",o:["He wanted to improve his Spanish.","He hoped it would help him choose a course.","His school organised the trip."],a:1,x:"‘I wasn't sure what to study… I thought it might help me decide.’"},{q:"What did Jack do in the village?",o:["He helped with English classes.","He taught sport.","He worked on a farm."],a:0,x:"‘helping the teachers with English lessons’"},{q:"What did Jack find most difficult?",o:["the food","the altitude","understanding people"],a:2,x:"‘It was the language… For the first month I understood almost nothing.’"},{q:"What does Jack say about the family he lived with?",o:["They wanted to learn English.","Their house was very small.","They were very kind to him."],a:2,x:"‘they were so generous. They treated me like a son.’"},{q:"What happened on Jack's last day?",o:["He gave a speech.","The children sang for him.","There was a party in the village."],a:1,x:"‘the children at the school sang a song they'd written for me’"},{q:"What is Jack going to do in September?",o:["go back to Peru","start teacher training","study Spanish"],a:1,x:"‘I'm going to train to be a primary school teacher. I start in September.’"}]}}}),LZ.data.lpapers.b1.push({id:"b1l5",title:"Listening Test 5",topics:"Everyday situations · A climbing wall · Opinions · A young chess player",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You will hear a girl talking to her mother about a lost item.",q:"Where did the girl find her keys?",o:["in her coat pocket","on the kitchen table","in her school bag"],a:0,x:"‘They were in the pocket of my coat the whole time!’ She had already looked on the table and in her bag.",script:[["F","Mum, have you seen my keys? I've looked on the kitchen table and I've emptied my school bag twice."],["M","Did you check your coat?"],["F","Oh! Yes, here they are. They were in the pocket of my coat the whole time!"]]},{ctx:"You will hear a man talking about his holiday.",q:"What was the weather like?",o:["rainy all week","very hot","cold but sunny"],a:2,x:"‘It was freezing in the mornings, but there wasn't a single cloud.’ He'd expected rain and it wasn't hot.",script:[["M","Everyone told us it would rain all week up there, so we packed umbrellas. In fact, it was freezing in the mornings, but there wasn't a single cloud in the sky. Not exactly beach weather, but perfect for walking."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a woman phoning a cinema.",q:"Which film will she see?",o:["the comedy","the space film","the animated film"],a:1,x:"‘Two tickets for the space film, then.’ The comedy is sold out and the cartoon is for younger children.",script:[["F","Hello, are there any tickets left for the comedy tonight?"],["M","I'm sorry, that's sold out. There's the new animated film at seven."],["F","Hmm, that's really for younger children, isn't it? What about the space film?"],["M","Yes, plenty of seats."],["F","Two tickets for the space film, then."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a boy talking about how he gets to school.",q:"How does he usually travel to school?",o:["by train","by bus","on foot"],a:0,x:"‘Most days I get the train.’ He walks only when it's sunny and the bus takes too long.",script:[["M","The bus takes nearly an hour because it stops everywhere, so I don't bother with that. When it's sunny, I sometimes walk, but it's quite far. Most days I get the train – it's only ten minutes."]]},{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a party.",q:"What will the boy bring to the party?",o:["some drinks","a cake","some music"],a:2,x:"‘Leave the music to me.’ Someone else is bringing drinks and the girl is making a cake.",script:[["F","So, for Saturday, Max is bringing the drinks and I'm making a chocolate cake."],["M","What can I do?"],["F","We haven't got anyone to play music yet."],["M","Leave the music to me – I've got a great speaker."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a woman talking to a shop assistant.",q:"Why does she want to return the shoes?",o:["They are the wrong size.","They are uncomfortable.","She doesn't like the colour."],a:1,x:"‘They hurt my feet after ten minutes.’ The size is right and she likes the colour.",script:[["F","I'd like to return these shoes, please. They're the right size, and I love the colour, but they hurt my feet after ten minutes of walking."],["M","No problem. Have you got the receipt?"]]},{ctx:"You will hear a man talking about a museum.",q:"What did he enjoy most at the museum?",o:["the dinosaur skeletons","the café","the gift shop"],a:0,x:"‘The dinosaur skeletons were the best part.’ The café was expensive and the shop was crowded.",script:[["M","The café was a bit expensive, and the gift shop was so crowded we didn't stay long. But the dinosaur skeletons were the best part – they were enormous. My son wants to go back next week."]]}]},2:{extracts:[{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a TV series.",q:"What does the girl think of the series?",o:["The story is too slow.","The actors are not very good.","It is better than she expected."],a:2,x:"‘I thought I'd hate it, but I'm really enjoying it.’",script:[["M","Are you still watching that detective series?"],["F","Yes! My brother made me start it. I thought I'd hate it, but I'm really enjoying it. I've watched five episodes this week."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a boy telling a friend about a new hobby.",q:"Why did he start playing the guitar?",o:["to join a band","to relax after school","because his parents wanted him to"],a:0,x:"‘Some friends are starting a band and they need a guitarist.’",script:[["F","Since when do you play the guitar?"],["M","Only since last month. Some friends of mine are starting a band, and they need a guitarist, so I thought I'd learn. It's harder than it looks!"]]},{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a restaurant.",q:"What does the man say about the restaurant?",o:["The food was cold.","The service was slow.","The prices were too high."],a:1,x:"‘We waited nearly an hour for our food.’ The food was good when it came, and it wasn't expensive.",script:[["F","How was the new Italian place?"],["M","The pizza was great when it finally came, and it wasn't expensive. But we waited nearly an hour for our food. I don't think I'll go back on a busy night."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a girl talking to her teacher about a project.",q:"How does the girl feel about her project?",o:["proud","worried","bored"],a:2,x:"‘I've been working on it for weeks and I'm just tired of it now.’",script:[["M","How's the history project going?"],["F","It's nearly finished. It's not bad, but honestly, I've been working on it for weeks and I'm just tired of it now. I can't wait to start something different."]]},{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a new sports centre.",q:"What do they agree about?",o:["The swimming pool is too small.","It is too expensive.","It is too far from town."],a:0,x:"‘The pool's tiny, though.’ – ‘I know, you can't really swim lengths.’ They disagree about the price.",script:[["M","I think the new sports centre's great value."],["F","Really? I think it costs too much. The pool's tiny, though, isn't it?"],["M","I know – you can't really swim lengths in it."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a woman talking about learning to drive.",q:"What does she find most difficult?",o:["driving at night","parking","driving on the motorway"],a:1,x:"‘Parking is my problem. I just can't get into small spaces.’",script:[["F","I'm quite happy driving on the motorway now, and night driving doesn't bother me. Parking is my problem. I just can't get into small spaces – I usually give up and find somewhere else."]]}]},3:{title:"The climbing wall",ctx:"You will hear a man talking about a new climbing wall at a sports centre.",script:[["M",`Hi, everyone. I'm Ryan, and I'm one of the instructors at the new climbing wall at Riverside Sports Centre. It opens on Saturday the twelfth of March, and it's the tallest indoor wall in the area – fifteen metres high.

If you've never climbed before, don't worry. Our beginners' sessions are every Tuesday after school, from half past four until six. An instructor stays with you the whole time.

You'll need trainers and comfortable clothes. We provide the special shoes and the safety equipment, so you don't need to buy anything.

A session costs eight pounds, but if you buy a card for ten sessions, it's sixty pounds.

You must be at least ten years old to climb, and anyone under sixteen needs a form signed by a parent. You can download it from our website.

At the end of the course there's a small competition, and the winner gets a free month of climbing.`]],items:[{s:"The climbing wall opens on {g} March.",a:["12th","12","twelfth","the twelfth","12th of","Saturday 12th"],x:"‘It opens on Saturday the twelfth of March’"},{s:"Beginners' sessions are on {g} after school.",a:["Tuesday","Tuesdays"],x:"‘Our beginners' sessions are every Tuesday after school’"},{s:"Climbers should wear trainers and comfortable {g}.",a:["clothes"],x:"‘You'll need trainers and comfortable clothes.’"},{s:"A card for ten sessions costs £{g}.",a:["60","sixty","£60"],x:"‘if you buy a card for ten sessions, it's sixty pounds’"},{s:"Climbers under sixteen need a {g} signed by a parent.",a:["form","parent form","permission form"],x:"‘anyone under sixteen needs a form signed by a parent’"},{s:"The competition winner gets a free {g} of climbing.",a:["month"],x:"‘the winner gets a free month of climbing’"}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a girl called Lily Chen, who plays chess for her country's junior team.",script:[["M","My guest today is fourteen-year-old Lily Chen, who's just been chosen for the national junior chess team. Lily, how did you start playing?"],["F","My grandfather taught me when I was six. He played every evening after dinner, and I used to sit and watch. One day he asked if I wanted to try, and I loved it straight away."],["M","How much do you practise?"],["F","About an hour every day, and more at weekends. But I don't just play games. I spend a lot of time studying famous games from the past, to see how the best players think."],["M","What's the hardest part of a competition?"],["F","People think it's the thinking, but for me it's sitting still for so long. A game can last four hours, and I'm quite a lively person! I've learned to take a short walk between moves."],["M","Do your friends play chess too?"],["F","Not really. Most of them think it's a bit boring. But when I won my first big competition, they all came to watch, which was really nice."],["M","What do you do when you lose a game?"],["F","I used to get really upset. Now I write down the moves and go through them with my coach the next day. You learn more from losing than from winning."],["M","And what's next for you?"],["F","The European junior championship in Italy in the summer. I'm excited, but I'm also a bit nervous because I've never travelled without my parents before."]],qs:[{q:"How did Lily start playing chess?",o:["Her grandfather taught her.","She learned at school.","She watched games online."],a:0,x:"‘My grandfather taught me when I was six.’"},{q:"As well as playing, Lily practises by",o:["playing against a computer.","reading books about chess.","studying famous old games."],a:2,x:"‘I spend a lot of time studying famous games from the past’"},{q:"What does Lily find hardest in a competition?",o:["thinking quickly","sitting still for hours","playing older people"],a:1,x:"‘for me it's sitting still for so long’"},{q:"What does Lily say about her friends?",o:["They often play chess with her.","They want to learn to play.","They came to see her win a competition."],a:2,x:"‘when I won my first big competition, they all came to watch’"},{q:"What does Lily do after she loses a game?",o:["She looks at the moves with her coach.","She plays another game straight away.","She takes a few days off."],a:0,x:"‘I write down the moves and go through them with my coach the next day.’"},{q:"How does Lily feel about going to Italy?",o:["She is only excited.","She is excited but nervous.","She is not looking forward to it."],a:1,x:"‘I'm excited, but I'm also a bit nervous’"}]}}}),LZ.data.lpapers.c1.push({id:"c1l1",title:"Listening Test 1",topics:"Three conversations · A sound designer · The value of boredom · Changing career",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You hear two friends discussing a documentary series about the ocean.",script:[["M","So, did you watch the rest of that ocean series in the end?"],["F","I did, and I was pleasantly surprised. I'd assumed it'd be the usual doom and gloom – dying reefs, sad music – and there was a bit of that, of course. But what really stood out was that they let the fishermen and the divers on those islands tell the story themselves. You don't often hear from the people whose livelihoods are actually on the line."],["M","Hmm, I'll grant you that. The science was pretty lightweight, though, wasn't it? Not that I minded."],["F","Well, it's hardly meant for marine biologists."],["M","Fair enough. My issue was more the ending. After fifty minutes of telling us how serious things are, it just… stopped. Not a word about what any of us could actually do about it. You're left feeling helpless, which surely isn't the point."],["F","I suppose they didn't want to lecture people."]],qs:[{q:"What does the woman feel the documentary did particularly well?",o:["It explained complex science in an accessible way.","It avoided exaggerating the threats facing the ocean.","It gave a voice to people who depend on the sea."],a:2,x:"‘What really stood out was that they let the fishermen and the divers… tell the story themselves.’ There was ‘a bit of’ doom and gloom, and the man calls the science ‘lightweight’."},{q:"The man thinks the documentary's main weakness was",o:["its reliance on dramatic images.","its lack of practical suggestions.","its narrow focus on one region."],a:1,x:"‘Not a word about what any of us could actually do about it.’ He ‘didn't mind’ the lightweight science."}]},{ctx:"You hear two colleagues talking about working in a shared office space.",script:[["F","I didn't realise you'd given up working from home altogether."],["M","Three months ago now. People assume it was the kids, but to be honest they're at school most of the day. It was more that I'd stopped being able to switch off – the laptop was on the kitchen table, so work just bled into everything. Now when I leave the building, that's it, I'm done. I did half hope I'd pick up some work from the people there, but that hasn't really happened."],["F","I've been going to the one near the station on Fridays. I'll be honest, I get far less done than I'd imagined – there's always someone wanting to chat, or a phone going off."],["M","Oh, tell me about it. I thought I'd be super-productive, and some days I barely write a line."],["F","Still, the coffee's decent."],["M","For what we pay, it ought to be!"]],qs:[{q:"Why did the man decide to rent a desk in a shared office?",o:["He wanted to separate his work from his home life.","He hoped to find new clients there.","His home had become too noisy to work in."],a:0,x:"‘I'd stopped being able to switch off… Now when I leave the building, that's it.’ Finding work was only a half-hope, and the children are at school."},{q:"What do they both think about the shared office?",o:["The facilities are not worth the cost.","It is harder to concentrate there than they expected.","The social side is its greatest benefit."],a:1,x:"She gets ‘far less done than I'd imagined’; he thought he'd be ‘super-productive’ but ‘some days I barely write a line’."}]},{ctx:"You hear two people talking about a novel they have both read.",script:[["M","You finished The Glass Harbour, then?"],["F","Last night. I have to say, I didn't warm to Nora at all to begin with – she's so cold with her sister. But by the last few chapters, when you understand what she gave up for the family, I found myself genuinely admiring her. I didn't see that coming."],["M","Same here. What did you make of the way it jumps around in time?"],["F","It drove me mad at first."],["M","I know people find it confusing, but I thought it was clever. Nora herself can't keep her memories in order – she's grieving, she's exhausted – so the book's all over the place because she is. It wouldn't work told in a straight line."],["F","I suppose that's true."]],qs:[{q:"What does the woman say about the novel's main character?",o:["She found her motives hard to believe.","She came to admire her as the novel went on.","She identified with her more than she had expected."],a:1,x:"‘I didn't warm to Nora at all to begin with… by the last few chapters… I found myself genuinely admiring her.’"},{q:"What is the man's opinion of the novel's structure?",o:["It is unnecessarily complicated.","It reflects the character's state of mind.","It makes the ending too predictable."],a:1,x:"‘Nora herself can't keep her memories in order… so the book's all over the place because she is.’"}]}]},2:{ctx:"You will hear a sound designer called Maya Brennan talking about her work in the film industry.",script:[["F",`Good evening. When I tell people I'm a sound designer, most of them assume I compose film music. I don't – that's a completely separate job. What I do is create everything else you hear: footsteps, doors, wind, the particular hum of a spaceship engine. And I got into it almost by accident. I'd trained as a radio journalist, and on my first placement I was sent to record a thunderstorm for a documentary. I spent the whole night on a hillside, and when I heard the playback, I realised I cared far more about the sound than the story.

People are often surprised to learn how little of what they hear in a film was recorded on set. On a typical drama, perhaps a third of the dialogue gets re-recorded later in a studio, and almost all the smaller sounds are added afterwards by people called Foley artists. They perform the sounds live while watching the film – walking on trays of gravel, rustling fabric, that kind of thing. One of my favourite tricks is that the sound of someone walking through snow is often made by squeezing a bag of cornflour. It sounds more like snow than real snow does.

The part of the job I find most demanding isn't the technical side, which you can learn. It's the patience. On my last film, I spent eleven days on a single scene – a two-minute sequence in a crowded market – layering nearly four hundred separate sounds so that it felt alive without drowning the conversation.

Silence, by the way, is one of the most powerful tools we have. But true silence on a cinema soundtrack actually makes audiences uncomfortable – they start to think the equipment has broken. So what we call silence is usually a very quiet background sound, known as room tone, which tells your brain you're still inside the world of the film.

Technology has changed things enormously. When I started, we cut magnetic tape by hand. Now I can build an entire forest on a laptop. The danger is that because everything is possible, sound can become over-designed – every leaf rustling, every breath exaggerated. The best advice I ever got came from an old editor who told me that sound should be felt rather than noticed.

If you're thinking of a career in this field, my advice is simple: start a library of your own recordings. Directors hire people whose sounds they can't find anywhere else. I still carry a small recorder everywhere I go – my family are used to me stopping in the street to record a squeaky gate.`]],items:[{s:"Maya originally trained to work as a {g}.",a:["radio journalist","journalist"],x:"‘I'd trained as a radio journalist’ – composing film music is a different job."},{s:"Maya discovered her interest in sound while recording a {g} at night.",a:["thunderstorm","storm"],x:"‘I was sent to record a thunderstorm for a documentary. I spent the whole night on a hillside.’"},{s:"In a typical drama, perhaps {g} of the dialogue is re-recorded in a studio.",a:["a third","one third","1/3","a third of it"],x:"‘perhaps a third of the dialogue gets re-recorded later in a studio’"},{s:"The sound of footsteps in snow is often made with a bag of {g}.",a:["cornflour","corn flour","cornstarch"],x:"‘walking through snow is often made by squeezing a bag of cornflour’ – gravel is used for other footsteps."},{s:"Maya says the most demanding aspect of her work is the {g} it requires.",a:["patience"],x:"‘It isn't the technical side… It's the patience.’"},{s:"Maya once spent eleven days on a scene set in a {g}.",a:["crowded market","market"],x:"‘a two-minute sequence in a crowded market’"},{s:"What sound designers call silence is usually a quiet sound known as {g}.",a:["room tone"],x:"‘a very quiet background sound, known as room tone’"},{s:"Maya advises newcomers to build a {g} of their own recordings.",a:["library","sound library"],x:"‘start a library of your own recordings’"}]},3:{ctx:"You will hear an interview in which a psychologist called Dr Adam Hale talks about boredom.",script:[["F","My guest today is the psychologist Dr Adam Hale, whose new book argues that boredom is not the enemy we think it is. Adam, why write a whole book about something so, well, dull?"],["M","I get that a lot. The honest answer is that I didn't set out to. I was researching attention – why we find it harder to concentrate than we used to – and I kept finding that the people who struggled most weren't the ones with the most distractions. They were the ones who couldn't tolerate even a few minutes of having nothing to do. Boredom kept turning up in the data, so eventually I stopped treating it as background noise and made it the subject."],["F","So what actually is boredom, from a psychologist's point of view?"],["M","The best definition I've found is that it's the uncomfortable feeling of wanting to be engaged in something satisfying and not being able to. It's not the same as having nothing to do – plenty of people sit on a beach doing nothing and feel perfectly content. Boredom is a kind of signal. It's your mind telling you that what you're doing isn't meeting your needs, and prompting you to change something."],["F","Then why do we try so hard to avoid it?"],["M","Because the signal is unpleasant, and now we have an instant way to switch it off. The phone in your pocket means you need never sit with the feeling for more than a second. The trouble is, that's a bit like taking painkillers for a broken leg without ever setting the bone. You feel better briefly, but nothing changes, and the restlessness comes back, often worse. In one of our studies, people who reached for their phones most often during a dull task reported feeling more bored at the end, not less."],["F","Your book has a chapter called ‘The Useful Void’. What's useful about it?"],["M","There's quite good evidence now that a period of boredom can boost creative thinking. In a well-known experiment, people who'd first done something mind-numbingly repetitive – copying numbers out of a phone directory – came up with more original uses for everyday objects afterwards than people who went straight to the task. I'd be cautious, though. The effect is real but modest, and it seems to depend on the mind being allowed to wander. If you're bored and anxious at the same time, you don't get any creative benefit at all."],["F","What about children? Parents often feel guilty if their kids are bored."],["M","I'd tell them to relax. Children who are constantly entertained by adults don't get the chance to discover what they actually enjoy. When my own daughter complains she's bored, I've learned not to rush in with suggestions. Nine times out of ten, twenty minutes later she's building something elaborate out of cardboard. The skill of occupying yourself is learned, and it can only be learned by being given the opportunity."],["F","Finally, has writing the book changed your own habits?"],["M","It has, a little. I used to fill every spare moment – podcasts on the walk to work, emails in the queue at the supermarket. Now I leave my phone in my bag on the train. The first week was genuinely hard; I hadn't realised how restless I'd become. But I've noticed I sleep better, and some of my better ideas for the book came while I was staring out of a train window. I won't pretend I've given up podcasts entirely, though."]],qs:[{q:"What led Adam to write about boredom?",o:["He wanted to challenge a popular theory about concentration.","It emerged unexpectedly from research on a related topic.","He had personal experience of long periods of boredom.","His publisher asked him to write about it."],a:1,x:"‘I didn't set out to. I was researching attention… Boredom kept turning up in the data.’"},{q:"How does Adam define boredom?",o:["as a state of complete mental inactivity","as a sign that someone lacks imagination","as a message that what we are doing is unfulfilling","as a reaction to being forced to wait"],a:2,x:"‘It's your mind telling you that what you're doing isn't meeting your needs.’ It's ‘not the same as having nothing to do’."},{q:"What point does Adam make with the comparison to painkillers?",o:["Phones deal with the symptom but not the underlying cause.","Some people become physically dependent on their phones.","Boredom can cause physical as well as mental discomfort.","Avoiding boredom is sometimes medically necessary."],a:0,x:"‘You feel better briefly, but nothing changes, and the restlessness comes back.’"},{q:"What is Adam's attitude to research linking boredom and creativity?",o:["He doubts whether the experiments were properly designed.","He believes its importance has been underestimated.","He accepts it but warns against overstating the effect.","He thinks it only applies to people in creative jobs."],a:2,x:"‘I'd be cautious, though. The effect is real but modest.’"},{q:"What does Adam suggest parents should do when children are bored?",o:["give them a range of activities to choose from","let them find their own way out of it","limit their use of screens during the day","explain to them why boredom is valuable"],a:1,x:"‘I've learned not to rush in with suggestions… The skill of occupying yourself… can only be learned by being given the opportunity.’"},{q:"How has Adam's behaviour changed since writing the book?",o:["He has stopped listening to podcasts altogether.","He now deliberately allows himself time without stimulation.","He has started taking the train instead of walking to work.","He finds it easier than before to concentrate on emails."],a:1,x:"‘Now I leave my phone in my bag on the train’ and stares out of the window. He hasn't ‘given up podcasts entirely’."}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear five short extracts in which people talk about changing career in their forties.",tasks:[{prompt:"For questions 21–25, choose from the list (A–H) what made each speaker decide to change career.",options:["a serious health problem","a conversation with a stranger","losing their previous job","a wish to spend more time outdoors","the example of a family member","frustration at a lack of recognition","a feeling of having achieved everything","an unexpected sum of money"]},{prompt:"For questions 26–30, choose from the list (A–H) what each speaker has found most challenging about their new career.",options:["being less experienced than colleagues","the physical demands of the work","earning far less money","other people's negative reactions","the length of the training","working irregular hours","having to learn new technology","a lack of job security"]}],speakers:[{a:[4,0],x:["E: ‘my aunt, at sixty-one, retrained as a midwife… what exactly is my excuse?’","A: ‘being on the ward with nurses half my age who knew infinitely more than I did’. The course itself ‘was fine’ (not E)."],script:[["F","I'd been an accountant for twenty years, and I was good at it, but I'd never have described it as a passion. Then my aunt, at sixty-one, retrained as a midwife, and I remember thinking – if she can start again at her age, what exactly is my excuse? So at forty-four I enrolled on a nursing course. The course itself was fine. What I found hardest, honestly, was being on the ward with nurses half my age who knew infinitely more than I did. I'd gone from being the person everyone asked to being the one asking all the questions."]]},{a:[1,3],x:["B: a furniture maker he sat next to on a delayed flight – ‘I went back to my desk… and couldn't stop thinking about it’.","D: ‘old colleagues who clearly thought I'd lost my mind… That wore me down more than any amount of sanding.’"],script:[["M","I was on a delayed flight, sitting next to a woman who turned out to be a furniture maker. We talked for three hours, and she described what it was like to make something with your hands that would outlast you. I went back to my desk at the bank on Monday and couldn't stop thinking about it. Within a year I'd handed in my notice. The workshop itself I love. What I wasn't prepared for was how people reacted – old colleagues who clearly thought I'd lost my mind, friends asking when I was going to get a proper job again. That wore me down more than any amount of sanding."]]},{a:[0,2],x:["A: ‘At forty-two I had a heart scare… The doctor was very blunt.’","C: ‘I earn about a quarter of what I used to.’"],script:[["F","At forty-two I had a heart scare – nothing fatal, thankfully, but it made me take a long look at the way I was living. Sixty-hour weeks in advertising, constant deadlines. The doctor was very blunt. So I retrained as a yoga teacher, which I'd done for years as a hobby. I'm healthier and far calmer, but I'd be lying if I said the money didn't bother me. I earn about a quarter of what I used to, and I've had to give up things I'd taken for granted, like holidays abroad."]]},{a:[5,5],x:["F: ‘every time a project went well, someone more senior took the credit… passed over for promotion for the third time’.","F: ‘the shifts – forecasting doesn't stop at night… plays havoc with my sleep’."],script:[["M","I'd spent fifteen years at a software company, and every time a project went well, someone more senior took the credit. After being passed over for promotion for the third time, I thought, enough. I'd always been fascinated by the weather, so I trained as a meteorologist. I'm valued now, which makes a huge difference. The one thing I still haven't adjusted to is the shifts. Forecasting doesn't stop at night, and working three nights in a row and then days again plays havoc with my sleep."]]},{a:[3,1],x:["D: ‘I'd barely seen daylight on a weekday since I was twenty-five. I wanted to work outside, simple as that.’","B: ‘nobody warned me how hard it is on the body… my back and knees let me know about it’."],script:[["F","I'd sat in the same grey office in the city for nineteen years, and I realised one spring that I'd barely seen daylight on a weekday since I was twenty-five. I wanted to work outside, simple as that. So I became a gardener – first on a big estate, now with my own small business. I adore it, but nobody warned me how hard it is on the body. At forty-seven, digging all day, my back and knees let me know about it every single evening."]]}]}}}),LZ.data.lpapers.c1.push({id:"c1l2",title:"Listening Test 2",topics:"Three conversations · Shipwreck archaeology · A zero-waste kitchen · A year abroad",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You hear two friends talking about an exhibition about robots.",script:[["M","Did you get to that robot exhibition in the end?"],["F","Saturday. I went expecting to spend ages on the bits where you can programme the robots yourself, but there were queues everywhere, so I gave up. What actually fascinated me was the first room – these clockwork figures from the eighteenth century that could write letters. I had no idea people were building that sort of thing so long ago."],["M","Oh, the writing boy! Yes, that was remarkable. I have to say, though, I came out feeling slightly manipulated."],["F","Manipulated?"],["M","Well, it was sponsored by a tech company, and it showed. Everything was about how robots will make our lives easier. Not one mention of the jobs they're already replacing. It felt more like an advert than an exhibition."],["F","I suppose I didn't think about who was paying for it."]],qs:[{q:"What did the woman find most interesting about the exhibition?",o:["the robots that visitors could control","the historical examples of early machines","the discussion of robots and employment"],a:1,x:"‘What actually fascinated me was the first room – these clockwork figures from the eighteenth century.’ She gave up on the interactive robots because of the queues."},{q:"The man criticises the exhibition for",o:["being aimed mainly at children.","being too crowded to enjoy.","presenting a one-sided view."],a:2,x:"‘Everything was about how robots will make our lives easier. Not one mention of the jobs they're already replacing.’"}]},{ctx:"You hear two neighbours talking about a plan to build new houses in their village.",script:[["F","Have you seen the notice about the forty new houses on the field behind the school?"],["M","I have. And look – my kids can't afford to live here; their friends have all moved to the city. Something has to be built somewhere. I'd rather it was done properly than not at all."],["F","I'm not against houses as such. But have you seen the drawings? Great big boxes with no gardens. They'll look completely out of place."],["M","They are a bit brutal."],["F","There's a consultation meeting next week. Rather than just objecting, I thought I'd take along some photos of the older cottages and propose they use similar materials and rooflines. They might listen if it's constructive."],["M","Good luck. Count me in if you need support."]],qs:[{q:"How does the man feel about the plan?",o:["resigned to it going ahead","angry that residents were not consulted","supportive of the need for new housing"],a:2,x:"‘My kids can't afford to live here… Something has to be built somewhere.’"},{q:"What does the woman intend to do?",o:["organise a petition against the plan","suggest changes to the design","attend a meeting just to find out more"],a:1,x:"‘Rather than just objecting… propose they use similar materials and rooflines.’ She is going to the meeting to make a proposal, not only to listen."}]},{ctx:"You hear a man and a woman talking about learning a musical instrument as an adult.",script:[["M","I hear you've taken up the piano."],["F","Eight months ago. Everyone assumes it's one of those keep-the-brain-young things, but no. My daughter plays the violin and she's got a concert next spring. Her teacher asked if anyone could play the piano part with her, and I thought, why not me? So I've got a deadline."],["M","That's lovely. I started the guitar at fifty. I tell you what, the practising isn't the problem – I love it. It's how painfully slowly things sink in. My son picked up in a month what's taken me a year."],["F","Oh, absolutely. I watch her learn a piece in a week and it takes me three. It's humbling."]],qs:[{q:"Why did the woman start learning the piano?",o:["to prove she could master a difficult skill","to be able to play alongside her daughter","to keep her mind active as she got older"],a:1,x:"Her daughter's teacher needed someone to play the piano part with her. She rejects the ‘keep-the-brain-young’ reason."},{q:"What do they agree about learning as an adult?",o:["Practising regularly is the hardest part.","Adults are more motivated than children.","Progress is slower than it is for children."],a:2,x:"He: ‘how painfully slowly things sink in’; she: ‘I watch her learn a piece in a week and it takes me three.’ He says the practising ‘isn't the problem’."}]}]},2:{ctx:"You will hear a marine archaeologist called Tom Reyes talking about his work on shipwrecks.",script:[["M",`Thanks for coming along tonight. I've been diving on shipwrecks for nearly twenty years, and I still get the same feeling every time I drop down through the water and a hull starts to appear out of the gloom. People imagine the job is all about treasure. In reality, the most valuable things we bring up are usually ordinary objects – a piece of rope, a sailor's shoe – things that tell us how people actually lived.

My route into the field was a little unusual. I studied history at university, but I'd never dived until I took a summer job as a dive instructor's assistant in Malta. By the end of that summer I'd seen my first wreck, and I knew exactly what I wanted to do.

The biggest challenge underwater isn't the depth or the cold. It's visibility. On some of the sites in the North Sea you can barely see your own hand, so we often work by touch, and we map the site using sound rather than sight.

Time is the other great enemy. At thirty metres, you might get just twenty minutes on the bottom before you have to begin the slow ascent. So every dive is planned to the minute, and each diver has one specific task.

Our most exciting recent project was a merchant ship that sank in 1702 off the Welsh coast. We'd expected to find cargo such as wine or cloth, but the hold was full of clay pipes – thousands of them, still packed in straw. They'd been made in Bristol and were on their way to Ireland.

Wood is our biggest headache once things have been raised. After three centuries underwater, timber is like a wet sponge – if you let it dry out naturally, it shrinks and cracks within hours. So everything goes straight into tanks of water, and conservation can take ten years or more.

Increasingly, we don't raise wrecks at all. We record them in extraordinary detail with 3D scanning and leave them where they are, which is often the best way to protect them. The scans also mean that people who will never dive can explore a wreck from their sofa.

And for anyone tempted to take up this work – learn to love paperwork. For every hour underwater, I spend about ten hours at a desk writing reports.`]],items:[{s:"Tom says the most valuable finds are usually {g} objects, such as rope.",a:["ordinary","everyday"],x:"‘the most valuable things we bring up are usually ordinary objects’ – not treasure."},{s:"Tom first dived while working as a {g}'s assistant in Malta.",a:["dive instructor","diving instructor"],x:"‘a summer job as a dive instructor's assistant in Malta’"},{s:"According to Tom, the biggest challenge underwater is {g}.",a:["visibility","poor visibility"],x:"‘The biggest challenge underwater isn't the depth or the cold. It's visibility.’"},{s:"At thirty metres, divers may have only {g} on the bottom.",a:["twenty minutes","20 minutes","20 mins","twenty mins"],x:"‘you might get just twenty minutes on the bottom’"},{s:"The ship that sank in 1702 was carrying thousands of {g}.",a:["clay pipes","pipes"],x:"‘the hold was full of clay pipes’ – wine and cloth were what they had expected."},{s:"The cargo was on its way from Bristol to {g}.",a:["Ireland"],x:"‘They'd been made in Bristol and were on their way to Ireland.’"},{s:"Tom compares wood that has been underwater for centuries to a {g}.",a:["wet sponge","sponge"],x:"‘timber is like a wet sponge’"},{s:"For every hour underwater, Tom spends about ten hours writing {g}.",a:["reports"],x:"‘I spend about ten hours at a desk writing reports.’"}]},3:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a chef called Nadia Osei, whose restaurant produces almost no waste.",script:[["M","My guest is Nadia Osei, whose London restaurant has become famous for producing almost no waste. Nadia, where did the idea come from?"],["F","Not from any grand principle, I'm afraid. When I opened the restaurant, money was desperately tight, and I was horrified by what we were throwing away every night – bags and bags of it. Every one of those bags was money I'd paid for. Reducing waste began as a way of surviving. The environmental argument came later, once I started reading about it."],["M","What did you change first?"],["F","The menu, which surprised people. Most chefs design a menu and then order whatever it needs. We turned that round. We see what the farmers have too much of that week – the misshapen carrots, a surplus of cabbages – and then we write the menu. It means the menu changes almost daily, which my staff found exhausting at first. Now they say it's the most creative part of the job."],["M","Some critics say zero-waste cooking is more about marketing than substance."],["F","I understand the scepticism. There are restaurants that put a few vegetable peelings on the plate and call themselves sustainable. But you can't fake it for long – the numbers don't lie. We weigh every bin, every day, and we publish the figures on our website. If people want to check, they can."],["M","What's the hardest thing to avoid wasting?"],["F","People assume it's food, but we've largely solved that – what we can't cook, we ferment or compost. It's packaging that defeats us. Some of our suppliers still deliver in plastic, and there's only so much you can persuade a small business to change when they're struggling themselves. I try not to be preachy about it."],["M","Do diners notice any of this?"],["F","Honestly, most don't, and I'm fine with that. They come because the food is good – or I hope so. If they left thinking about waste rather than about what they'd eaten, I'd feel I'd failed as a cook. The sustainability should be invisible, really. It's the kitchen's job, not the customer's."],["M","What's next for you?"],["F","I've been asked many times to open a second restaurant, and I've always said no. What I'm doing instead is running free courses for other chefs – twelve so far this year. One restaurant can only do so much. If a hundred kitchens each cut their waste by half, that's worth far more than anything I could achieve alone."]],qs:[{q:"What originally motivated Nadia to reduce waste?",o:["a documentary she had seen about food waste","the need to cut the restaurant's costs","pressure from her customers","her training at a previous restaurant"],a:1,x:"‘Every one of those bags was money I'd paid for. Reducing waste began as a way of surviving.’ The environmental argument ‘came later’."},{q:"How did Nadia's staff react to the new approach to the menu?",o:["They felt it limited their creativity.","They resisted it until customers responded well.","They were enthusiastic from the start.","They found it tiring at first but came to enjoy it."],a:3,x:"‘my staff found exhausting at first. Now they say it's the most creative part of the job.’"},{q:"How does Nadia respond to criticism of zero-waste restaurants?",o:["She admits that her restaurant has sometimes exaggerated its claims.","She accepts that some criticism is fair but says her results are open to checking.","She suggests that critics do not understand how kitchens work.","She believes marketing is a necessary part of the movement."],a:1,x:"‘I understand the scepticism… We weigh every bin, every day, and we publish the figures.’"},{q:"What does Nadia find most difficult to eliminate?",o:["food that customers leave on their plates","ingredients that cannot be preserved","packaging from suppliers","energy used in the kitchen"],a:2,x:"‘It's packaging that defeats us.’ Food waste is ‘largely solved’."},{q:"What is Nadia's view of her customers' awareness of her methods?",o:["She wishes more of them understood what she is trying to do.","She thinks they should be encouraged to reduce their own waste.","She feels it does not matter as long as they enjoy the food.","She worries that it spoils their experience."],a:2,x:"‘Most don't, and I'm fine with that… The sustainability should be invisible.’"},{q:"Why is Nadia running courses for other chefs?",o:["to earn extra income for her restaurant","because she has decided to stop cooking herself","to have a wider impact than one restaurant can","because other restaurants have copied her ideas badly"],a:2,x:"‘One restaurant can only do so much. If a hundred kitchens each cut their waste by half…’ The courses are free."}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear five short extracts in which people talk about spending a year living abroad.",tasks:[{prompt:"For questions 21–25, choose from the list (A–H) the main reason each speaker gives for moving abroad.",options:["to study a subject not available at home","to escape a stressful situation","to join a partner","to take up a job offer","to learn a language properly","to look after a relative","to fulfil a childhood ambition","to save money"]},{prompt:"For questions 26–30, choose from the list (A–H) what surprised each speaker most about living abroad.",options:["how quickly they felt at home","how much they missed familiar food","how different the working culture was","how hard it was to make local friends","how much the weather affected their mood","how expensive daily life was","how their view of their own country changed","how little they used the language they had learned"]}],speakers:[{a:[4,3],x:["E: ‘I'd done six years of Portuguese at school and could barely order a coffee, so… the only way to really learn it was to live in Lisbon.’","D: ‘getting invited into anyone's home… that took months. Most of my friends that year were other foreigners.’"],script:[["M","I'd done six years of Portuguese at school and could barely order a coffee, so I decided the only way to really learn it was to live in Lisbon for a year. I found a room, a part-time job in a bookshop, and I made myself speak it all day. What caught me off guard was the social side. People were perfectly friendly at work, but getting invited into anyone's home, getting beyond polite chat – that took months. Most of my friends that year were other foreigners, which wasn't the plan at all."]]},{a:[2,4],x:["C: ‘My husband got a post at a university in Helsinki, and I followed him.’","E: ‘what months of near-darkness does to you… my mood was on the floor’. The cold ‘was fine’."],script:[["F","My husband got a post at a university in Helsinki, and I followed him – I work freelance, so I could work from anywhere. I'd prepared myself for the cold, and actually the cold was fine. What nobody tells you is what months of near-darkness does to you. By December I was sleeping ten hours and still exhausted, and my mood was on the floor. Then in May the light came back and I felt like a different person."]]},{a:[3,2],x:["D: ‘I was headhunted by an engineering firm in Tokyo – the salary was too good to turn down.’","C: ‘What really threw me was the office… the unwritten rules.’ Food and language were ‘no problem’."],script:[["M","I was headhunted by an engineering firm in Tokyo – the salary was too good to turn down. I'd visited Japan as a tourist, so I thought I knew what to expect. Food, language, trains – no problem. What really threw me was the office. Meetings where the real decision had clearly been made beforehand, colleagues who wouldn't leave until the boss did. It took me a long time to understand the unwritten rules."]]},{a:[1,6],x:["B: ‘a divorce, a business that went under – I needed to get away from everything that reminded me of it.’","G: ‘I came back seeing it through their eyes… It's never looked quite the same since.’"],script:[["F","I'd just come out of a very difficult few years – a divorce, a business that went under – and I needed to get away from everything that reminded me of it. A friend had a flat in Buenos Aires she wasn't using. I went for a year and did very little except walk and read. The strange thing was coming home. I'd spent twelve months hearing people talk about my country, and I came back seeing it through their eyes – the good and the not-so-good. It's never looked quite the same since."]]},{a:[6,0],x:["G: ‘Ever since I was about eight… I'd wanted to live there.’","A: ‘within about three weeks I felt like I'd lived there all my life’. He had expected to be homesick."],script:[["M","Ever since I was about eight and saw a documentary about the Canadian Rockies, I'd wanted to live there. At forty I finally did it – a year's contract at a ski resort in Alberta. I'd expected to feel homesick, especially at Christmas. But honestly, within about three weeks I felt like I'd lived there all my life. I had a local coffee shop; people knew my name. I nearly didn't come back."]]}]}}}),LZ.data.lpapers.c1.push({id:"c1l3",title:"Listening Test 3",topics:"Three conversations · Urban beekeeping · A documentary photographer · Life without a car",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You hear two colleagues talking about working from home.",script:[["F","So, three months of working from home. How are you finding it?"],["M","Honestly? The work itself is going better than ever. No interruptions, no one wandering over for a chat just as I've got into something. My output has probably doubled."],["F","But?"],["M","But I hadn't realised how much of what I learnt came from overhearing things. Just sitting near the senior people, hearing how they handled a difficult client on the phone. You don't get that on a video call."],["F","I know what you mean. For me it's the opposite, though. I'm constantly distracted at home – the kids, the washing, the fridge. I've actually started coming in two days a week just to get something done."]],qs:[{q:"What does the man miss about working in the office?",o:["having a clear boundary between work and home","the social life with his team","the chance to learn informally from colleagues"],a:2,x:"‘how much of what I learnt came from overhearing things… hearing how they handled a difficult client’. He doesn't miss the chats – they were interruptions."},{q:"The woman has started going into the office because",o:["her manager asked her to.","she finds it easier to concentrate there.","she wants to see her colleagues."],a:1,x:"‘I'm constantly distracted at home… I've started coming in two days a week just to get something done.’"}]},{ctx:"You hear a man and a woman discussing a novel they have both read.",script:[["M","Did you finish it?"],["F","Last night. I have to say, I was ready to give up after the first fifty pages – all that description of the town, the weather, the history of the family. But once the brother came back, I couldn't put it down."],["M","Same here. Though I thought the ending let it down. After all that tension, everything was tied up so neatly. Everyone forgiven, everyone happy. Real families don't work like that."],["F","Hmm. I'd have agreed with you if it hadn't been for the last page. Did you notice that the mother never actually speaks to him again? I think the author's telling us it's not as resolved as it looks."],["M","Oh. I must have missed that."]],qs:[{q:"What did the woman initially dislike about the novel?",o:["its complicated plot","its unlikeable characters","its slow opening"],a:2,x:"‘I was ready to give up after the first fifty pages – all that description’."},{q:"What does the woman suggest about the ending?",o:["It was unrealistic, as the man says.","It was predictable from the start.","It was more ambiguous than the man thinks."],a:2,x:"‘the mother never actually speaks to him again… it's not as resolved as it looks.’"}]},{ctx:"You hear two friends talking about running a marathon.",script:[["F","I can't believe you're actually doing a marathon. You used to hate running!"],["M","I still don't love it, to be honest. But my dad had a heart operation last year, and the hospital was incredible. The race is raising money for their cardiac unit. That's what gets me out of bed at six."],["F","How's the training going?"],["M","My legs are coping fine – I'm quite surprised. It's my head that's the problem. Around the twenty-kilometre mark I start telling myself I can't do it, and it takes everything I've got to keep going. I've started listening to podcasts, which helps a bit."],["F","Well, I'll be at the finish line with a sign."]],qs:[{q:"Why is the man running the marathon?",o:["to raise funds for a hospital","to get fit after an illness","to prove something to his father"],a:0,x:"‘The race is raising money for their cardiac unit. That's what gets me out of bed.’ It was his father who was ill."},{q:"What does he find hardest about training?",o:["getting up early","staying mentally motivated","coping with injuries"],a:1,x:"‘My legs are coping fine… It's my head that's the problem.’"}]}]},2:{ctx:"You will hear a woman called Clara Finch talking about keeping bees on city rooftops.",script:[["F",`Good evening, everyone. I'm Clara Finch, and for the last eight years I've kept bees on the roofs of office buildings in the centre of Manchester. People are often amazed that bees can survive in a city at all. In fact, they tend to do better here than in the countryside, because city gardens and parks offer a far greater variety of flowers than fields planted with a single crop.

I got into beekeeping almost by accident. I was working as an accountant, and our building manager wanted to put hives on the roof as part of a green initiative. Nobody else volunteered, so I did. Within a year, I'd left my job.

The hardest part of rooftop beekeeping isn't the bees – it's the wind. At twenty storeys up, a strong gust can knock a hive over, so every one of ours is strapped down with heavy straps.

People always ask whether the honey tastes of pollution. It doesn't. Bees filter out almost everything, and our honey has been tested by a laboratory every year. What it does taste of is lime trees, which line many of the city's streets – in July it's quite distinctive.

One of our biggest successes has been with schools. We've taken more than three thousand children onto the rooftops, and for most of them it's the first time they've seen where honey actually comes from.

I should add a warning, though. Rooftop hives have become fashionable, and some cities now have more bees than the flowers can support. That's bad news for wild bees, which have to compete for the same food. So before setting up a hive, find out what else is in your area.

If you'd like to get involved, the best first step is to join a local beekeeping association. Most of them run a beginners' course in spring, and many will lend you equipment for your first season.`]],items:[{s:"City bees can find a greater {g} of flowers than those in the countryside.",a:["variety","range","diversity"],x:"‘city gardens and parks offer a far greater variety of flowers’"},{s:"Before becoming a beekeeper, Clara worked as an {g}.",a:["accountant"],x:"‘I was working as an accountant’"},{s:"The biggest problem for rooftop hives is the {g}.",a:["wind","strong wind","winds"],x:"‘The hardest part of rooftop beekeeping isn't the bees – it's the wind.’"},{s:"Every hive is secured with heavy {g}.",a:["straps"],x:"‘every one of ours is strapped down with heavy straps’"},{s:"In July, the honey tastes of {g}.",a:["lime trees","lime","limes","lime tree"],x:"‘What it does taste of is lime trees… in July it's quite distinctive.’"},{s:"More than {g} children have visited the rooftop hives.",a:["3,000","3000","three thousand"],x:"‘We've taken more than three thousand children onto the rooftops’"},{s:"Too many hives in a city can be harmful to {g}.",a:["wild bees","wild bee"],x:"‘That's bad news for wild bees, which have to compete for the same food.’"},{s:"Many associations will lend beginners {g} for their first season.",a:["equipment","kit","their equipment"],x:"‘many will lend you equipment for your first season’"}]},3:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a documentary photographer called Daniel Mercer.",script:[["F","My guest is Daniel Mercer, whose photographs of fishing communities have just been published as a book. Daniel, how did the project start?"],["M","I'd been commissioned by a magazine to take a single portrait of a trawlerman in Cornwall – a one-day job. But when I got there, he told me his was the last boat in the harbour, and that in ten years there'd be no fishing there at all. I realised I was looking at something that was disappearing, and I felt someone ought to record it properly. That one day turned into four years."],["F","You spent a lot of time at sea with the fishermen. Was that difficult?"],["M","Physically, yes – I was seasick for the first six trips. But the real challenge was earning their trust. They'd had journalists before who'd portrayed them as either heroes or dinosaurs. For the first few months I barely took any pictures at all. I just helped with the nets and kept my mouth shut."],["F","Your photographs are all in black and white. Why?"],["M","People assume it's to make them look timeless or romantic, and I'm actually rather wary of that. The real reason is practical. On deck, the light changes every few seconds, and the colours were a mess – orange oilskins, blue nets, green water. In black and white, you see the faces and the work. The colour was getting in the way."],["F","Some critics have said the book is too sad."],["M","I can see why. There's a lot of loss in it. But I'd say it's a book about pride more than sadness. These people do an extraordinarily hard job with enormous skill. If readers only come away feeling sorry for them, then I haven't done my job."],["F","Has the book changed anything?"],["M","It's too early to say whether it'll make any difference to fishing policy – I'm not naive about that. But something unexpected has happened. Young people from the town have been in touch saying they'd never really understood what their grandparents did, and several have asked to go out on the boats. That's worth more to me than any review."],["F","What's next?"],["M","I'm going to do the same thing with a mining community in Wales. But this time I'm going to hand out cameras to the miners' families as well. I want the people in the pictures to have a say in how they're shown."]],qs:[{q:"What made Daniel decide to extend his original assignment?",o:["The magazine asked him to produce a longer feature.","He realised a way of life was coming to an end.","The fisherman invited him to stay in the town.","He wanted to challenge negative images of fishing."],a:1,x:"‘his was the last boat in the harbour… I realised I was looking at something that was disappearing.’"},{q:"What does Daniel say was most difficult about working with the fishermen?",o:["coping with seasickness","learning to help with the fishing","gaining their confidence","taking photographs in bad weather"],a:2,x:"‘the real challenge was earning their trust’. Seasickness was only physical and short-lived."},{q:"Why did Daniel choose to work in black and white?",o:["to give the images a romantic quality","because colour distracted from what mattered","because it was cheaper to print","to make the book look like older photographs"],a:1,x:"‘In black and white, you see the faces and the work. The colour was getting in the way.’ He is wary of the romantic reading."},{q:"How does Daniel respond to the criticism that the book is too sad?",o:["He suggests critics have misunderstood the fishermen.","He admits he should have included more positive images.","He says sadness was unavoidable given the subject.","He argues that its main theme is something else."],a:3,x:"‘I'd say it's a book about pride more than sadness.’"},{q:"What has pleased Daniel most since the book came out?",o:["its influence on government policy","the positive reviews it has received","the interest shown by young local people","the fishermen's reaction to their portraits"],a:2,x:"‘Young people from the town… have asked to go out on the boats. That's worth more to me than any review.’"},{q:"What will Daniel do differently in his next project?",o:["work more quickly","involve the community in taking the photographs","use colour photography","focus on a single family"],a:1,x:"‘this time I'm going to hand out cameras to the miners' families as well.’"}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear five short extracts in which people talk about giving up their car.",tasks:[{prompt:"For questions 21–25, choose from the list (A–H) the main reason each speaker gives for getting rid of their car.",options:["the cost of running it","concern about pollution","moving to a city centre","being involved in an accident","wanting to get fitter","pressure from a partner","the car breaking down","starting a job close to home"]},{prompt:"For questions 26–30, choose from the list (A–H) what has surprised each speaker most since giving up their car.",options:["how much time they have saved","how much fitter they have become","how well they now know their local area","how complicated shopping has become","how much money they have saved","how often friends offer them lifts","how reliable public transport is","how rarely they miss having a car"]}],speakers:[{a:[0,2],x:["A: ‘the insurance went up for the third year running, and the parking permit… doubled… I simply couldn't justify it any more.’","C: ‘I know the names of half the shopkeepers on my street now… I'd been driving past all of it for years.’"],script:[["F","When the insurance went up for the third year running, and the parking permit for my street doubled on top of that, I simply couldn't justify it any more. The car was sitting outside my house six days a week. I assumed I'd spend my first months car-free counting the money I was saving, but honestly, I hardly think about that. What's struck me is that I now walk everywhere, and I know the names of half the shopkeepers on my street. There's a bakery two minutes away I'd been driving past for years without noticing."]]},{a:[3,6],x:["D: ‘after I went into the back of someone at a roundabout… I just didn't trust myself behind the wheel.’","G: ‘I'd always assumed the buses round here were hopeless… they've let me down once in a year.’"],script:[["M","Nobody was hurt, thank goodness, but after I went into the back of someone at a roundabout, something changed. I just didn't trust myself behind the wheel any more, and I sold the car a month later. My wife thought I'd buy another within weeks. I'd always assumed the buses round here were hopeless – everyone complains about them – but they've let me down once in a year. Once! I've actually started defending them at dinner parties, which I never expected."]]},{a:[1,1],x:["B: ‘I read a report about the air on the road outside my children's school… I couldn't keep adding to it.’","B: ‘I've lost six kilos without even trying… my doctor was amazed.’"],script:[["F","I read a report about the quality of the air on the road outside my children's school, and it genuinely frightened me. I was one of the parents sitting in a queue of cars with the engine running every morning, and I couldn't keep adding to it. So we got cargo bikes instead. I'd expected it to be exhausting, and at first it was. But I've lost six kilos without even trying, and at my last check-up my doctor was amazed at my blood pressure. That was a lovely surprise."]]},{a:[2,3],x:["C: ‘when we moved into a flat right in the centre… there was nowhere to park it anyway’.","D: ‘The weekly shop is the real headache… it's become a major operation.’"],script:[["M","It wasn't a principled decision, I'm afraid. When we moved into a flat right in the centre of town, there was nowhere to park anyway – the nearest space was a twenty-minute walk and cost a fortune. So it went. Getting to work is easier than ever. The weekly shop is the real headache, though. You can't carry a week's food home on foot, so it's either several trips or ordering online and waiting in for a delivery. It's become a major operation."]]},{a:[6,7],x:["G: ‘the engine finally died on the motorway… I decided not to replace it’.","H: ‘I thought I'd be desperate for one within a month… I honestly can't remember the last time I wished I had it.’"],script:[["M","My old car's engine finally died on the motorway one Friday evening, and while I was waiting for the recovery truck, I thought: do I really need another one? I decided not to replace it, just to see. I thought I'd be desperate for one within a month – I'd driven since I was seventeen. But I honestly can't remember the last time I wished I had it. I rent one for the odd weekend away, and that's it. Friends keep asking when I'm buying a new one, and the answer is: probably never."]]}]}}}),LZ.data.lpapers.c1.push({id:"c1l4",title:"Listening Test 4",topics:"Three conversations · Restoring old maps · A sleep scientist · Learning something new",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You hear two friends talking about a restaurant they have just visited.",script:[["M","Well? Was it worth the three-month wait for a table?"],["F","The food, yes, absolutely. That lamb dish was extraordinary. But I couldn't relax. The waiters hovered over us the whole time, explaining every ingredient in great detail. I just wanted to talk to you."],["M","I didn't mind that, actually – I found it quite interesting. What got to me was the bill. I don't object to paying for good food, but there was a fifteen per cent charge added on top that nobody mentioned when we booked."],["F","Really? I didn't even look."]],qs:[{q:"What spoilt the meal for the woman?",o:["the quality of the food","the attentiveness of the staff","the time she had to wait for a table"],a:1,x:"‘The waiters hovered over us the whole time… I just wanted to talk to you.’ The food was ‘extraordinary’."},{q:"What was the man unhappy about?",o:["the overall price of the meal","a charge that wasn't made clear in advance","the explanations given by the waiters"],a:1,x:"‘I don't object to paying for good food, but there was a fifteen per cent charge… that nobody mentioned.’"}]},{ctx:"You hear two students discussing a group project.",script:[["F","How's the project going with your group?"],["M","Don't ask. There are four of us, and two of them have done absolutely nothing. I've ended up writing most of the report myself."],["F","Have you told the tutor?"],["M","I thought about it, but I don't want to look like I'm complaining. And it's partly my own fault – I took over too quickly at the start because I was worried about the deadline. They probably thought I didn't want their help."],["F","Maybe. But you still need to sort it out before the presentation. You can't do that on your own."],["M","True. I'm going to ask them each to take one section of the talk. At least that way it's clear who's responsible."]],qs:[{q:"How does the man feel about the situation?",o:["He blames the tutor for creating the groups.","He accepts he has contributed to the problem.","He is determined to report the others."],a:1,x:"‘it's partly my own fault – I took over too quickly at the start’."},{q:"What does the man decide to do?",o:["ask the tutor for more time","prepare the presentation himself","give each member a specific task"],a:2,x:"‘I'm going to ask them each to take one section of the talk.’"}]},{ctx:"You hear a man and a woman talking about a TV documentary about climate change.",script:[["F","Did you see the documentary about the glaciers last night?"],["M","I did. The photography was stunning, but I'm not sure what they were trying to achieve. It was just an hour of bad news. By the end I felt so helpless I switched it off."],["F","I know people say that, but I think the problem is we're not scared enough. If a programme like that makes people uncomfortable, good."],["M","But people don't act when they're frightened – they switch off, like I did. If they'd shown some of the things that are actually working, people might feel they could do something."],["F","Hmm. Maybe there's room for both."]],qs:[{q:"What was the man's reaction to the documentary?",o:["He felt it exaggerated the problem.","He found it too depressing to continue watching.","He was impressed by its message."],a:1,x:"‘By the end I felt so helpless I switched it off.’"},{q:"The man thinks documentaries on this subject should",o:["focus on the scientific evidence.","include examples of successful solutions.","avoid showing distressing images."],a:1,x:"‘If they'd shown some of the things that are actually working, people might feel they could do something.’"}]}]},2:{ctx:"You will hear a man called Oliver Grant talking about his work restoring old maps.",script:[["M",`Hello, everyone. I'm Oliver Grant, and I restore historic maps – mostly for libraries and private collectors, occasionally for museums. The oldest map I've worked on was drawn in 1480, and it had spent the previous century folded up inside a book, which is why it survived at all.

I trained originally as a bookbinder, and that's still the most useful background for this work, because so many old maps were bound into atlases. Understanding how paper was folded and stitched tells you a great deal about a map's history.

The most common damage I see isn't from age at all. It's from earlier repairs. For much of the twentieth century, people fixed tears with sticky tape, and that tape turns yellow and brittle over time and leaves a stain that's almost impossible to remove.

To clean a map, I often use nothing more than a soft brush and a special kind of rubber. The golden rule of restoration is that anything I do must be reversible, so that a restorer in a hundred years' time can undo my work if they find a better method.

Maps are also fascinating for their mistakes. One seventeenth-century map I restored showed California as an island. That error was copied by mapmakers for nearly a hundred years, simply because nobody went to check.

My favourite part of the job, though, is finding things that aren't meant to be there. People wrote notes in the margins, and I once found a list of shopping on the back of a map of Scotland, dated 1790.

The work is slow. A single large map can take me six months. And the market has changed: collectors now want maps in their original condition, and they'll often pay more for a map with honest damage than for one that's been over-restored.

If you own an old map, please don't frame it in direct sunlight. Light fades the colours faster than anything else.`]],items:[{s:"The oldest map Oliver has restored survived because it was kept inside a {g}.",a:["book"],x:"‘it had spent the previous century folded up inside a book’"},{s:"Oliver's original training was as a {g}.",a:["bookbinder","book binder"],x:"‘I trained originally as a bookbinder’"},{s:"The most common damage is caused by earlier repairs using {g}.",a:["sticky tape","tape"],x:"‘people fixed tears with sticky tape’ – not by age."},{s:"Oliver's golden rule is that all restoration must be {g}.",a:["reversible"],x:"‘anything I do must be reversible’"},{s:"A seventeenth-century map wrongly showed {g} as an island.",a:["California"],x:"‘One seventeenth-century map I restored showed California as an island.’"},{s:"On the back of one map, Oliver found a {g} from 1790.",a:["shopping list","list of shopping"],x:"‘I once found a list of shopping on the back of a map of Scotland, dated 1790.’"},{s:"A single large map can take up to {g} to restore.",a:["six months","6 months"],x:"‘A single large map can take me six months.’"},{s:"Maps should not be framed in {g}.",a:["direct sunlight","sunlight"],x:"‘please don't frame it in direct sunlight’"}]},3:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a sleep scientist called Dr Helen Park.",script:[["M","My guest today is Dr Helen Park, who has spent twenty years studying sleep. Helen, what first drew you to the subject?"],["F","It was very personal, actually. I was a junior doctor working thirty-six-hour shifts, and I made a mistake with a patient's medication – thankfully caught in time. It shook me. I started reading about what lack of sleep does to decision-making, and I never really stopped."],["M","We hear a lot about needing eight hours. Is that right?"],["F","It's a useful average, but it's not a rule. Some people genuinely need seven, some need nine. What matters more than the exact number is regularity – going to bed and getting up at roughly the same time, even at weekends. That's the thing most people get wrong."],["M","What about phones in the bedroom?"],["F","Everyone blames the blue light from screens, and it does have some effect. But in our research, the bigger problem was what people were doing on their phones. Reading work emails or arguing on social media at eleven at night winds you up. A calm film on a tablet is far less harmful than one stressful message."],["M","Is it true that teenagers are naturally lazy about getting up?"],["F","No, and that's a myth I'd love to get rid of. During adolescence the body clock genuinely shifts later by two hours or so. Asking a sixteen-year-old to be alert at eight in the morning is like asking an adult to be alert at six. It's biology, not laziness. Some schools that have started later have seen real improvements in results."],["M","Do you follow your own advice?"],["F","Mostly. I'm very strict about my bedtime. The one thing I struggle with is caffeine – I love a coffee after lunch, and I know perfectly well it's still in my system at bedtime. I'm working on it."],["M","What would you like to see change?"],["F","I'd like employers to take sleep as seriously as they take safety. We'd never let someone operate machinery after four glasses of wine, but we routinely let exhausted people make decisions that affect others. Being awake for twenty hours has a similar effect on performance."]],qs:[{q:"What led Helen to study sleep?",o:["a course she took as a medical student","an incident when she was working as a doctor","her own long-term sleep problems","a patient who suffered from insomnia"],a:1,x:"‘I made a mistake with a patient's medication… It shook me.’"},{q:"According to Helen, what is the most common mistake people make?",o:["sleeping fewer than eight hours","sleeping too much at weekends","not keeping a regular sleep routine","going to bed too early"],a:2,x:"‘What matters more… is regularity… That's the thing most people get wrong.’"},{q:"What did Helen's research find about phones?",o:["Blue light is the main cause of poor sleep.","The content people engage with matters more than the screen.","Watching films before bed is as harmful as reading emails.","Phones should never be kept in the bedroom."],a:1,x:"‘the bigger problem was what people were doing on their phones’. Blue light has only ‘some effect’."},{q:"What point does Helen make about teenagers?",o:["They need more sleep than adults.","Their behaviour is caused by a natural change in their body.","Schools should reduce their homework.","They use technology more than adults do."],a:1,x:"‘During adolescence the body clock genuinely shifts later… It's biology, not laziness.’"},{q:"What does Helen admit about her own habits?",o:["She often goes to bed late.","She finds it hard to give up afternoon coffee.","She works too late in the evening.","She uses her phone in bed."],a:1,x:"‘The one thing I struggle with is caffeine – I love a coffee after lunch.’ She's ‘very strict about my bedtime’."},{q:"What change would Helen most like to see?",o:["more research into sleep disorders","shorter working hours for doctors","employers treating tiredness as a safety issue","later start times for all schools"],a:2,x:"‘I'd like employers to take sleep as seriously as they take safety.’"}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear five short extracts in which people talk about learning a new skill as an adult.",tasks:[{prompt:"For questions 21–25, choose from the list (A–H) what each speaker learnt.",options:["a musical instrument","a foreign language","sailing","pottery","computer programming","dancing","painting","cooking"]},{prompt:"For questions 26–30, choose from the list (A–H) what each speaker has gained from the experience.",options:["new friendships","more patience","a new source of income","greater self-confidence","a better relationship with a family member","a way of relaxing","a new career direction","a different view of failure"]}],speakers:[{a:[3,7],x:["D: ‘my bowls collapsed on the wheel every single time’.","H: ‘I used to be terrified of getting things wrong… now I actually enjoy the mistakes’."],script:[["F","I signed up for a pottery class on a whim. For the first six weeks, my bowls collapsed on the wheel every single time, and everyone else's looked perfect. I nearly quit. But the teacher kept saying, ‘Clay remembers everything – just start again.’ In my job, I used to be terrified of getting things wrong. Now I actually enjoy the mistakes, because that's where you learn something. It's changed the way I work, too."]]},{a:[1,4],x:["B: ‘I decided to learn Italian so I could talk to my father-in-law.’","E: ‘Now we chat for hours… we're closer than we've ever been.’"],script:[["M","My wife's father is Italian and speaks very little English, and for twenty years our conversations were basically smiling and nodding. So at forty-five I decided to learn Italian so I could actually talk to him. It was hard – my memory isn't what it was. But now we chat for hours on the phone about football and politics. We're closer than we've ever been, and he tells everyone his English son-in-law speaks like a Roman."]]},{a:[4,6],x:["E: ‘I did an online coding course in the evenings’.","G: ‘I'm starting a job as a junior developer next month.’"],script:[["F","I'd worked in a shop for years and I was bored, so I did an online coding course in the evenings, mainly out of curiosity. I found I was good at it – it's like solving puzzles all day. I made a couple of small apps for friends, and then a company saw one and asked if I'd like to apply. I'm starting a job as a junior developer next month. I'd never have imagined it."]]},{a:[5,0],x:["F: ‘salsa classes… I've got two left feet’.","A: ‘the people in that class are now some of my closest friends’."],script:[["M","After my divorce I was lonely, and my sister practically forced me to go to salsa classes. I've got two left feet, and I'm still not much of a dancer, to be honest. But that's not really the point. We go out for a meal after every class, we've been on holiday together – the people in that class are now some of my closest friends. I'd never have met them otherwise."]]},{a:[2,5],x:["C: ‘I took up sailing… on the lake near my house’.","F: ‘It's the only time my mind goes completely quiet.’"],script:[["F","I have a very stressful job in a hospital, and a colleague suggested I try sailing on the lake near my house. I thought it would be terrifying, but it's the opposite. Out there, you have to concentrate on the wind and the water, and there's no room for anything else. It's the only time my mind goes completely quiet. I go every Sunday, whatever the weather."]]}]}}}),LZ.data.lpapers.c1.push({id:"c1l5",title:"Listening Test 5",topics:"Three conversations · Drystone walling · A food historian · People who influenced us",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You hear two colleagues talking about a change to their working arrangements.",script:[["F","Have you read the email? From next month we're all expected in the office four days a week."],["M","I saw it. Honestly, I'm not as bothered as everyone else seems to be. Working from my kitchen table for three years hasn't exactly been glamorous."],["F","It's not the office I mind. It's the journey. That's nearly three hours a day I'll be spending on a train, and nobody's offering to pay for the tickets."],["M","Fair point. What annoys me is the way they announced it. No consultation, no explanation, just an email on a Friday afternoon. If they'd asked us, most people would probably have agreed to three days."],["F","You should say that at the staff meeting."]],qs:[{q:"What is the woman's main objection to the change?",o:["having to work in the office again","losing the freedom to choose her hours","the time and cost of travelling"],a:2,x:"‘It's not the office I mind. It's the journey… nearly three hours a day… and nobody's offering to pay for the tickets.’"},{q:"What does the man criticise?",o:["how the decision was communicated","the number of office days required","the conditions of working from home"],a:0,x:"‘What annoys me is the way they announced it. No consultation, no explanation, just an email.’"}]},{ctx:"You hear two friends discussing a film based on a novel they have both read.",script:[["M","So, did the film live up to the book?"],["F","In some ways it was better. I always found the middle of the novel rather slow, all those chapters about the family's history. The film cut most of that, and it moved along much faster."],["M","That's exactly what I missed, though. Those chapters explain why the two brothers can't stand each other. Without them, the big argument at the end just seems to come out of nowhere."],["F","Maybe. But you can't expect a two-hour film to include everything."],["M","No, but they found time for three car chases that weren't in the book at all."]],qs:[{q:"What did the woman like about the film?",o:["how closely it followed the novel","its faster pace","the way it showed the brothers"],a:1,x:"‘The film cut most of that, and it moved along much faster.’"},{q:"The man feels that the film",o:["should have been longer than two hours.","added scenes that were too violent.","failed to explain the characters' conflict."],a:2,x:"‘Without them, the big argument at the end just seems to come out of nowhere.’"}]},{ctx:"You hear a man and a woman talking about using an app that records their exercise.",script:[["F","Are you still using that running app?"],["M","I deleted it last week. At first I loved it. Seeing the graphs go up every week really got me out of the door. But after a while I was only running to beat my own numbers. I stopped enjoying the actual running."],["F","I know what you mean. I've switched off all the notifications on mine. I still record my runs, but I only look at them once a month."],["M","That sounds sensible. I might go back to it like that."],["F","The bit I'd never give up is the route map, though. I'd be lost in the woods without it."]],qs:[{q:"Why did the man stop using the app?",o:["It made him focus too much on his performance.","It pushed him to run more often than he wanted.","It gave him inaccurate information."],a:0,x:"‘after a while I was only running to beat my own numbers. I stopped enjoying the actual running.’"},{q:"What does the woman value most about her app?",o:["its monthly progress reports","its maps of where she runs","its reminders to exercise"],a:1,x:"‘The bit I'd never give up is the route map.’ She has switched off the notifications."}]}]},2:{ctx:"You will hear a woman called Kate Morley talking about her work building drystone walls.",script:[["F",`Hi, I'm Kate Morley, and I build drystone walls, mostly in the north of England. A drystone wall is built without any cement at all. The stones hold each other in place by their own weight and the way they're fitted together.

I didn't come from a farming family. I was working as an accountant in Leeds when my brother gave me a weekend course as a birthday present, and by the Sunday evening I'd decided to change my life.

People imagine the hardest part is lifting heavy stones. It isn't. The real skill is choosing the right stone for each place without picking it up twice. Experienced wallers say that if you handle a stone more than once, you've wasted your energy.

Every wall is really two walls leaning against each other, with small stones packed in the middle. We call that filling the hearting, and if it's done badly, the wall will bulge and fall within a few years.

The most important stones go along the top. They're called copes, and they're set on their edge to stop sheep from climbing over.

A well-built wall can last two hundred years without repair. Surprisingly, the biggest threat isn't the weather. It's tree roots, which slowly push the stones apart from underneath.

I work outdoors in every season except the depths of winter, because frost makes the ground too hard and the stones too slippery to handle safely. On a good day I can build about three metres of wall, which means moving roughly a tonne of stone.

And the thing I love most? Finding objects buried in walls I'm repairing. I once found a clay pipe that a waller must have dropped about a hundred and fifty years ago.`]],items:[{s:"Before becoming a waller, Kate worked as an {g}.",a:["accountant"],x:"‘I was working as an accountant in Leeds’"},{s:"Kate's first walling course was a {g} from her brother.",a:["birthday present","present"],x:"‘my brother gave me a weekend course as a birthday present’"},{s:"A skilled waller tries not to pick up a stone {g}.",a:["twice","more than once"],x:"‘without picking it up twice’"},{s:"The small stones packed inside the wall are called the {g}.",a:["hearting"],x:"‘We call that filling the hearting’"},{s:"The stones along the top stop {g} from climbing over.",a:["sheep"],x:"‘set on their edge to stop sheep from climbing over’"},{s:"According to Kate, the biggest threat to a wall is {g}.",a:["tree roots","roots"],x:"‘the biggest threat isn't the weather… It's tree roots’"},{s:"Kate stops working in deep winter because of {g}.",a:["frost"],x:"‘frost makes the ground too hard and the stones too slippery’"},{s:"Inside one old wall, Kate found a {g}.",a:["clay pipe","pipe"],x:"‘I once found a clay pipe that a waller must have dropped’"}]},3:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a food historian called Marcus Bell.",script:[["F","My guest today is Marcus Bell, a food historian who recreates meals from centuries ago. Marcus, how did you get into this?"],["M","By accident, really. For my doctorate I was studying letters written by servants in a country house, and I kept coming across descriptions of what they'd cooked. I got curious about whether the dishes would actually work, so I tried one at home. It was a disaster, but I was hooked."],["F","What's the hardest thing about following old recipes?"],["M","People assume it's finding the ingredients, but most of those are surprisingly easy to get. The real problem is that old recipes leave so much out. They'll say ‘add enough flour’ or ‘bake until it's done’, because the writer assumed you already knew how to cook. So a lot of my work is educated guesswork."],["F","Were people's diets in the past as bad as we think?"],["M","It depends enormously on who you were. Wealthy families ate huge amounts of meat and sugar, and their teeth suffered terribly. But ordinary farm workers often had a fairly healthy diet: plenty of vegetables, beans and bread. What they lacked was variety, especially in late winter, when the stores were running low."],["F","Is there a dish you'd love to bring back?"],["M","There's a spiced pudding made with breadcrumbs that was eaten all over the country in the seventeenth century. It's cheap, it uses up stale bread, and it's genuinely delicious. I've served it to guests without telling them how old it is, and they always ask for the recipe."],["F","Do people ever react badly to your work?"],["M","Occasionally. Some people find it strange that I'll spend three days making a single pie. But the criticism I take more seriously comes from other historians, who point out that I can never truly know how something tasted. They're right, of course. Our sense of taste is shaped by everything else we eat, so even if I follow a recipe perfectly, I'm tasting it as a modern person."],["F","Finally, what do you hope people take away from your work?"],["M","Mainly, that cooking has always been creative. We tend to imagine people in the past following tradition without question, but the old recipe books are full of crossings-out and notes like ‘better with less salt’. People were experimenting all the time, just as we do."]],qs:[{q:"How did Marcus become interested in historical food?",o:["He grew up in a family of professional cooks.","He was asked to cook for a museum event.","He came across it during his academic research.","He inherited a collection of old recipe books."],a:2,x:"‘For my doctorate I was studying letters… and I kept coming across descriptions of what they'd cooked.’"},{q:"What does Marcus say is the main difficulty with old recipes?",o:["They don't give precise instructions.","The ingredients are hard to find today.","They are written in unfamiliar language.","They were meant for very large households."],a:0,x:"‘The real problem is that old recipes leave so much out.’ The ingredients are ‘surprisingly easy to get’."},{q:"What point does Marcus make about the diet of ordinary people in the past?",o:["It was healthier than most diets today.","It contained too much sugar.","It depended on meat from their own animals.","It was limited in range at certain times of year."],a:3,x:"‘What they lacked was variety, especially in late winter’. The sugar was eaten by wealthy families."},{q:"Why would Marcus like the spiced pudding to become popular again?",o:["It has an unusual history.","It is economical and tastes good.","It is quicker to make than modern desserts.","It was a favourite of famous people."],a:1,x:"‘It's cheap, it uses up stale bread, and it's genuinely delicious.’"},{q:"Which criticism of his work does Marcus accept?",o:["His methods take too long to be practical.","He relies on modern equipment.","He chooses dishes that are too unusual.","He cannot experience food as people did in the past."],a:3,x:"‘I can never truly know how something tasted. They're right, of course.’"},{q:"What does Marcus most want people to understand?",o:["People have always adapted and experimented with recipes.","Traditional methods produce better results.","Old recipes should be kept in their original form.","Modern cooks depend too much on written recipes."],a:0,x:"‘cooking has always been creative… People were experimenting all the time, just as we do.’"}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear five short extracts in which people talk about someone who changed the way they work.",tasks:[{prompt:"For questions 21–25, choose from the list (A–H) who influenced each speaker.",options:["a former teacher","a neighbour","a manager","a customer","a grandparent","a younger colleague","a sports coach","a stranger"]},{prompt:"For questions 26–30, choose from the list (A–H) what each speaker learnt from this person.",options:["to ask for help","to listen more carefully","to take risks","to manage money","to be honest about mistakes","to say no","to stay calm under pressure","to keep things simple"]}],speakers:[{a:[3,4],x:["D: ‘I made a mess of a job for a retired couple’, and the husband spoke to her.","E: ‘I've owned up straight away ever since.’"],script:[["F","When I'd just started my own business fitting kitchens, I made a mess of a job for a retired couple. I'd measured one wall wrongly and the units didn't fit. My first instinct was to make excuses about the house being old. But the husband just looked at me and said, ‘Everyone gets things wrong. What people remember is whether you admit it.’ So I did, and I fixed it at my own cost. They've recommended me to about twenty people since, and I've owned up straight away ever since."]]},{a:[5,1],x:["F: ‘a graduate joined my team, half my age’.","B: ‘Now I let people finish, and I hear far more.’"],script:[["M","I'd been running a team for fifteen years when a graduate joined us, half my age. In meetings I'd always prided myself on being decisive, but she'd sit quietly and then ask one question that showed she'd heard what everyone really meant, not just what they said. My own boss had told me for years that I interrupted too much, and I'd ignored him. Watching her, I finally understood. Now I let people finish, and I hear far more."]]},{a:[0,2],x:["A: ‘My old art teacher from school got in touch’.","C: ‘I'd left my job and gone freelance… I've never regretted jumping.’"],script:[["F","My old art teacher from school got in touch out of the blue when I was thirty, after seeing some of my drawings online. I was working in insurance, safe and well paid, and she asked me why I'd never tried illustration professionally. My grandmother had always said the same thing, but somehow hearing it from her was different. Within a year I'd left my job and gone freelance. It's been scary at times, but I've never regretted jumping."]]},{a:[6,6],x:["G: ‘my swimming coach when I was a teenager’.","G: ‘I still do it today when the screen is full of planes’."],script:[["M","I'm an air traffic controller, and people assume my training taught me how to cope with pressure. It helped, but the person who really taught me was my swimming coach when I was a teenager. Before races I used to panic, and she'd make me count my breaths, slowly, in and out. I still do it today when the screen is full of planes and everyone's talking at once. Nobody would ever guess my heart is racing."]]},{a:[1,5],x:["B: ‘The woman who lived next door to me’.","F: ‘she turned down at least half of what she was asked to do… These days I decline things’."],script:[["F","The woman who lived next door to me when I first moved to the city ran a small charity. She was the busiest person I've ever met, yet she never seemed stressed. I asked her how, and she told me she turned down at least half of what she was asked to do, politely but firmly. I used to agree to every request at work and then stay until nine every night. These days I decline things without feeling guilty, and my evenings are my own again."]]}]}}}),LZ.data.lpapers.c1.push({id:"c1l6",title:"Listening Test 6",topics:"Three conversations · Mapping coral reefs · A children's book translator · Working fewer hours",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You hear two members of a book club discussing the novel they have just read.",script:[["F","I have to say, I struggled with this one. It took me three weeks to get through it."],["M","Really? I couldn't put it down. Though I'll admit the first fifty pages were hard going, with all those characters introduced at once."],["F","That wasn't my problem, actually. I just didn't believe the main character would give up everything to move to that island. Nothing in her past suggested she'd do it."],["M","I suppose the author wanted it to come as a surprise."],["F","Maybe. But a surprise still has to make sense afterwards. I think we should pick something lighter next month, to be honest."]],qs:[{q:"Why did the woman find the novel difficult?",o:["There were too many characters at the start.","The main character's decision wasn't convincing.","The plot was hard to follow."],a:1,x:"‘I just didn't believe the main character would give up everything… Nothing in her past suggested she'd do it.’ The characters at the start were the man's complaint."},{q:"What does the woman suggest for the next meeting?",o:["reading a book by the same author","discussing the ending again","choosing a less serious book"],a:2,x:"‘I think we should pick something lighter next month.’"}]},{ctx:"You hear two colleagues talking about changes to their office.",script:[["M","Have you seen the plans? They're taking down all the walls on our floor and putting everyone in one big space."],["F","I know. The idea is that we'll talk to each other more."],["M","Well, I need to concentrate for most of my day. I can't see how that's going to work with forty people on the phone around me."],["F","They've promised some quiet rooms you can book."],["M","If there are enough of them. What really bothers me is that nobody asked us. The first we heard of it was a drawing pinned to the notice board."]],qs:[{q:"What is the man's main worry about the new office?",o:["It will be too noisy to concentrate.","There won't be enough desks.","It will be hard to book meeting rooms."],a:0,x:"‘I need to concentrate… I can't see how that's going to work with forty people on the phone around me.’"},{q:"What does the man criticise about the decision?",o:["the cost of the changes","the lack of consultation","the timing of the work"],a:1,x:"‘What really bothers me is that nobody asked us.’"}]},{ctx:"You hear a man and a woman talking about a charity run they took part in.",script:[["F","So, how are your legs today?"],["M","Don't ask. But it was worth it. I raised nearly six hundred pounds, which is far more than I expected."],["F","That's brilliant. I was a bit disappointed with my time, though. I'd trained for months and I was slower than last year."],["M","It was so hot, though. Everyone was slower. And the main thing is the money for the hospital, isn't it?"],["F","I know, you're right. I'll stop complaining. Same again next year?"],["M","Ask me when I can walk down the stairs."]],qs:[{q:"How does the man feel about the run?",o:["pleased with how much money he raised","disappointed with his time","worried about an injury"],a:0,x:"‘I raised nearly six hundred pounds, which is far more than I expected.’ The disappointing time was the woman's."},{q:"What does the man say to the woman?",o:["She should have trained harder.","The weather affected everyone's result.","She should try a shorter race."],a:1,x:"‘It was so hot, though. Everyone was slower.’"}]}]},2:{ctx:"You will hear a marine biologist called Dr Sara Malik talking about her work mapping coral reefs.",script:[["F",`Good evening. I'm Sara Malik, and for the last ten years I've been making detailed maps of coral reefs in the Indian Ocean. People often assume I spend all my time diving. In fact, most of the mapping is done from a small boat using sound, which bounces off the seabed and tells us its shape.

I do dive, of course, usually twice a day, to check what the instruments show. On each dive I carry a waterproof camera fixed to a frame, so that every photograph covers exactly one square metre of reef.

The most useful information doesn't come from the coral at all, but from the fish. Counting certain species tells us very quickly whether a reef is healthy.

When I started, I expected the biggest threat to be warmer water. That is serious, but on the reefs I study, the more immediate problem is sand washed down from farms by the rivers, which settles on the coral and blocks out the light.

One thing that surprised me was how quickly a damaged reef can start to recover. On one site, after the local community agreed to stop fishing there, we saw young coral returning within three years.

A lot of our work now depends on volunteers. Local divers send us their photographs through an app, and we now receive about two thousand pictures a month.

If you want to help, the simplest thing you can do is choose a sunscreen without the chemicals that harm coral. And if you dive, please never touch the reef, however tempting it is.`]],items:[{s:"Most of the mapping is done from a boat using {g}.",a:["sound"],x:"‘most of the mapping is done from a small boat using sound’"},{s:"Sara usually dives {g} a day.",a:["twice","two times"],x:"‘usually twice a day’"},{s:"Each photograph shows exactly one {g} of reef.",a:["square metre","square meter"],x:"‘every photograph covers exactly one square metre of reef’"},{s:"Counting certain {g} shows whether a reef is healthy.",a:["fish","species","fish species"],x:"‘Counting certain species tells us very quickly whether a reef is healthy.’ – the fish."},{s:"On Sara's reefs, the most immediate threat is {g} from farms.",a:["sand"],x:"‘the more immediate problem is sand washed down from farms by the rivers’ – not warmer water."},{s:"Young coral returned within three years after people stopped {g} there.",a:["fishing"],x:"‘after the local community agreed to stop fishing there, we saw young coral returning within three years’"},{s:"Volunteers send about {g} photographs a month.",a:["2,000","2000","two thousand"],x:"‘we now receive about two thousand pictures a month’"},{s:"Sara asks people to choose a coral-safe {g}.",a:["sunscreen","sun cream"],x:"‘choose a sunscreen without the chemicals that harm coral’"}]},3:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a translator of children's books called Nina Hartley.",script:[["M","With me today is Nina Hartley, who translates children's books from German and Swedish into English. Nina, how did you get into translation?"],["F","Rather late, actually. I was a primary school teacher for fifteen years. I'd always read German books to improve my language, and one day I found a picture book I loved that didn't exist in English. I translated it for my class, a publisher saw it, and that was that."],["M","Is translating for children easier than translating for adults?"],["F","Everyone assumes so, because the sentences are shorter. But a short sentence leaves you nowhere to hide. In a picture book, every word has to work with the illustration and sound right when it's read aloud. I read every page out loud dozens of times before I'm happy with it."],["M","What do you do with jokes and wordplay?"],["F","That's the real challenge. A pun in Swedish almost never works in English. I don't try to translate the joke itself. I try to work out what effect it has on the child, whether they laugh or feel clever, and then I write a new joke that does the same thing."],["M","Do authors mind you changing things?"],["F","Most are delighted, as long as I explain why. One author did ask me to keep a character's name that was impossible to pronounce in English. We compromised by adding a little scene where another character struggles to say it too."],["M","What do you think about the number of translated books for children in Britain?"],["F","It's still tiny, around three per cent. Publishers say translated books don't sell. But I think that's partly because they're rarely promoted. When one is given a proper chance, children don't care where it comes from."],["M","Finally, what advice would you give someone who wants to start?"],["F","Read as much children's writing in your own language as you can. People think the key skill is the foreign language, but what editors look for first is someone who writes well in English."]],qs:[{q:"How did Nina become a translator?",o:["She studied translation at university.","A publisher asked her to translate a series.","She worked abroad as a teacher.","She translated a book for her pupils."],a:3,x:"‘I translated it for my class, a publisher saw it, and that was that.’"},{q:"What does Nina say about translating for children?",o:["It is easier because the sentences are short.","Short sentences make mistakes more obvious.","Illustrations make the text less important.","Children's books need more research."],a:1,x:"‘a short sentence leaves you nowhere to hide.’ Everyone else assumes it's easier."},{q:"How does Nina deal with jokes?",o:["She creates new ones with the same effect.","She explains them in a note.","She leaves them in the original language.","She removes them from the text."],a:0,x:"‘I try to work out what effect it has on the child… and then I write a new joke that does the same thing.’"},{q:"What happened with the author who wanted to keep a name?",o:["Nina refused to keep it.","The author changed the name himself.","They found a solution together.","The publisher made the final decision."],a:2,x:"‘We compromised by adding a little scene where another character struggles to say it too.’"},{q:"Why does Nina think translated children's books sell badly?",o:["Children prefer stories set in their own country.","Publishers rarely promote them.","They are more expensive to produce.","Parents don't know they exist."],a:1,x:"‘I think that's partly because they're rarely promoted.’"},{q:"What advice does Nina give new translators?",o:["Spend time living in another country.","Specialise in one foreign language.","Contact as many editors as possible.","Concentrate on writing well in English."],a:3,x:"‘what editors look for first is someone who writes well in English.’"}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear five short extracts in which people talk about deciding to work fewer hours.",tasks:[{prompt:"For questions 21–25, choose from the list (A–H) why each speaker decided to work fewer hours.",options:["to look after a relative","to start a business","to study for a qualification","after a health problem","to spend more time with young children","to do voluntary work","because their employer offered it","to train for a sporting event"]},{prompt:"For questions 26–30, choose from the list (A–H) what each speaker has noticed since then.",options:["They sleep better.","They get more done at work.","They have less money than they expected.","Their colleagues treat them differently.","They feel less guilty.","They have rediscovered an old interest.","Their relationship with a partner has improved.","They are more patient with people."]}],speakers:[{a:[6,1],x:["G: ‘my company started a trial of a four-day week’.","B: ‘I actually finish more in four days than I used to in five.’"],script:[["M","I'd never have asked for it myself, to be honest. But last year my company started a trial of a four-day week, same pay, and anyone could join. I thought I'd fall behind. The strange thing is, I actually finish more in four days than I used to in five, because I stop wasting time in meetings that don't need me. My manager's thinking of making it permanent."]]},{a:[0,7],x:["A: ‘my father's memory started to go… I wanted to be the one looking after him’.","H: ‘I used to get so irritated in queues… Now I just wait.’"],script:[["F","When my father's memory started to go, I didn't want strangers caring for him all day, so I cut my hours to three days a week. It's been hard, and of course there's less money. But something in me has changed. I used to get so irritated in queues or when someone at work was slow to understand. Now I just wait. Spending time with Dad has taught me that."]]},{a:[2,5],x:["C: ‘to do a part-time law degree’.","F: ‘I've started playing the piano again, which I hadn't touched since school.’"],script:[["M","I went down to four days so I could do a part-time law degree. I expected every spare minute to go on studying, and a lot of it does. But the odd thing is that having less time has made me use it better. I've even started playing the piano again, which I hadn't touched since I was at school. I'd forgotten how much I loved it."]]},{a:[4,4],x:["E: ‘when our twins were born’.","E: ‘I used to feel bad all the time… that feeling has gone.’"],script:[["F","When our twins were born, I went part-time. Before that, I was working long days and I felt bad all the time, at work because I wasn't at home, and at home because I'd brought work with me. Now my days are clearly divided. It's still exhausting, believe me, but that constant feeling that I was letting someone down has gone."]]},{a:[3,0],x:["D: ‘I had a minor heart attack at forty-five’.","A: ‘for the first time in years I sleep through the night’."],script:[["M","I had a minor heart attack at forty-five, and my doctor was very clear: carry on as you are, and the next one won't be minor. So I cut my hours. I was worried I'd be bored, but I've filled the time with walking and cooking. And for the first time in years I sleep right through the night, instead of lying awake going through my emails in my head."]]}]}}}),LZ.data.lpapers.b1.push({id:"b1pl1",practice:!0,title:"Practice set 1",topics:"Everyday situations · A cheese-making day · Opinions · A young drummer",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You will hear a girl talking to her father about her phone.",q:"Where did she leave her phone?",o:["at her friend's house","at the swimming pool","on the bus"],a:0,x:"‘I used it to call you from Lucy's house… Then I put it down on her kitchen table.’ The pool and the bus are only her dad's and her first guesses.",script:[["F","Dad, have you seen my phone? I had it at the swimming pool this morning, I'm sure."],["M","Did you check your sports bag? Or maybe you dropped it on the bus."],["F","No, wait – I remember now. I used it to call you from Lucy's house after lunch. Then I put it down on her kitchen table while we were making pancakes."],["M","Well, I'll ring Lucy's mum, then."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a man phoning a restaurant.",q:"What does he want to do?",o:["book a table for more people","cancel his booking","change the time of his booking"],a:2,x:"‘Could we come at eight instead?’ He says ‘we're still coming, and it's still four of us’, so A and B are wrong.",script:[["M","Hello, I booked a table for four people tonight, under the name Harris."],["F","Yes, Mr Harris – seven o'clock."],["M","That's right. We're still coming, and it's still four of us, but my wife's train is late. Could we come at eight instead?"],["F","Eight is fine. See you then."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a woman talking about a weekend away.",q:"What was the weather like?",o:["sunny","windy","rainy"],a:1,x:"‘The problem was the wind – it was so strong that we couldn't even put the tent up.’ They ‘didn't see a single drop’ of rain and not much sun.",script:[["F","We were worried, because the forecast said it would rain all weekend. In the end we didn't see a single drop, but we didn't see much sun either. The problem was the wind – it was so strong that we couldn't even put the tent up on the first night, so we slept in the car!"]]},{ctx:"You will hear a boy talking to a shop assistant about a T-shirt.",q:"What is the problem with the T-shirt?",o:["It's the wrong colour.","It's too small.","It's damaged."],a:1,x:"‘It's a medium, and I really need a large. It's too tight round the arms.’ He loves the colour and says it isn't damaged.",script:[["M","Excuse me, I bought this T-shirt here last week. Well, my aunt did – it was a present."],["F","Is there something wrong with it? Is it damaged?"],["M","No, it's fine, and I love the colour. But it's a medium, and I really need a large. It's too tight round the arms."],["F","No problem. Let me find you a large."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a teacher talking to a class.",q:"What will the students do first tomorrow?",o:["have a test","watch a film","go to the library"],a:0,x:"‘We'll do the vocabulary test first thing instead, and watch the film in the afternoon.’ The library books go back on Friday.",script:[["F","Before you go, there's a change to tomorrow's plan. I know I said we'd start with the film, but the screen in our classroom won't be repaired until lunchtime. So we'll do the vocabulary test first thing instead, and watch the film in the afternoon. And don't forget, your library books need to go back on Friday."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a man talking to his neighbour.",q:"What does he ask his neighbour to do?",o:["feed his cat","water his plants","take in a parcel"],a:2,x:"‘I'm expecting a parcel on Thursday. Would you mind taking it in for me?’ His sister will feed the cat and water the plants.",script:[["M","Hi, Mrs Lee. Sorry to bother you. I'm away until Sunday, and I'm expecting a parcel on Thursday. Would you mind taking it in for me?"],["F","Of course. Do you need me to feed the cat as well?"],["M","No, my sister's going to come round for that, and she'll water the plants too. Just the parcel. Thanks so much."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a girl talking about her new hobby.",q:"What is her new hobby?",o:["painting","photography","singing"],a:1,x:"‘For my birthday I got a camera, and now I go out every weekend taking pictures.’ Painting is what she used to do, and singing is her friends' idea.",script:[["F","My mum thought I'd start painting again, because I used to love it, and my friends wanted me to join their singing group. But for my birthday I got a camera, and now I go out every weekend taking pictures of birds and old buildings. I've even entered a competition!"]]}]},2:{extracts:[{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a museum they visited.",q:"What did the boy think of the museum?",o:["It was too crowded.","The guide was very interesting.","It was hard to find the way round."],a:1,x:"‘She was brilliant – she told us stories about every painting.’ It was busy ‘but not too bad’, and the guide kept them together in the confusing building.",script:[["F","Did you enjoy the museum?"],["M","Yes, more than I expected. It was busy, but not too bad. The building's a bit confusing, but the guide kept us all together. And she was brilliant – she told us stories about every painting, and some of them were really funny."],["F","Sounds great. Maybe I'll go next week."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a girl telling a friend about a new sports centre.",q:"What does she like most about it?",o:["the long opening hours","the cheap prices","the modern equipment"],a:0,x:"‘It's open from six in the morning until eleven at night, so I can go whenever I want.’ It ‘isn't cheap’, and the machines are ‘not that different’.",script:[["M","Have you been to the new sports centre yet?"],["F","Yes, twice. The machines in the gym are new, of course, but they're not that different from the old ones. And it isn't cheap. But it's open from six in the morning until eleven at night, so I can go whenever I want – even after my late shift at the café."],["M","That's useful."]]},{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a TV series.",q:"What do they agree about?",o:["The first episode was boring.","The main actor was excellent.","The ending was surprising."],a:2,x:"She ‘didn't expect that at all’ and he says ‘What a surprise!’ They disagree about the main actor and the first episode.",script:[["F","Did you watch the last episode? I didn't expect that at all!"],["M","Me neither. I was sure the brother was the thief. What a surprise!"],["F","I thought the main actor was a bit weak in some scenes, though."],["M","Really? I thought he was great. I found the first episode slow, but after that it was brilliant."],["F","Oh, I loved the first episode."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a man telling a friend about his new flat.",q:"How does he feel about it?",o:["pleased with where it is","worried about the cost","disappointed with the size"],a:0,x:"‘The best thing is it's right next to the park and only five minutes from work.’ He knew it was small and doesn't mind, and the rent is ‘about the same as before’.",script:[["F","How's the new flat?"],["M","Great. It's not big – the kitchen's tiny – but I knew that before I moved in, so I don't mind. The rent's about the same as before. The best thing is it's right next to the park and only five minutes from work. I can walk everywhere."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a boy talking to his mother about a school trip.",q:"Why doesn't he want to go on the trip?",o:["It costs too much.","His friends aren't going.","He has been there before."],a:2,x:"‘We went there with Grandad last summer… I've seen everything already.’ It's ‘only twenty pounds’, and his friends Sam and Leo are going.",script:[["F","Have you signed up for the trip to the science park?"],["M","I don't think I'll go, Mum."],["F","Why not? It's only twenty pounds, and Sam and Leo are going."],["M","I know. But we went there with Grandad last summer, remember? I've seen everything already. I'd rather wait for the next trip, to the castle."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a woman telling a friend about a cookery book.",q:"What does she like about the book?",o:["The recipes are all from one country.","The photos are very helpful.","It was very cheap."],a:1,x:"‘What I really love is that there's a photo for every step.’ It was a present, so she doesn't know the price.",script:[["M","Is that the cookery book you got for your birthday?"],["F","Yes. There are dishes from all over the world, and I was worried the recipes would be complicated, but they're actually quite simple. What I really love is that there's a photo for every step, so you can see exactly what it should look like."],["M","It looks expensive."],["F","I've no idea how much it cost – it was a present!"]]}]},3:{title:"Cheese-making Day",ctx:"You will hear a man called Rob Fletcher talking on the radio about a cheese-making day at his family's farm.",script:[["M",`Good morning! I'm Rob Fletcher, and my family has a small dairy farm about ten kilometres outside the town. This summer, for the first time, we're opening the farm to visitors for a cheese-making day.

The day starts at half past nine in our old barn, which we've turned into a kitchen. Please don't go to the farmhouse – just follow the yellow signs from the car park. First, you'll meet our cows. We have forty of them, and they all have names. Then you'll learn how we make three different kinds of cheese. The one people love most is a soft cheese with garlic, but you'll also try a hard cheese which is made with apple.

Lunch is included – it's homemade soup and bread, with our cheese of course. In the afternoon, everyone makes their own small cheese to take home. You'll need to keep it in the fridge for two weeks before you eat it, so be patient!

The day costs thirty-five pounds for adults and twenty pounds for children over eight. We ask everyone to wear old shoes or boots, because the farmyard can be very muddy. To book, please send us an email – the address is on our website.`]],items:[{s:"The day takes place in an old {g} on the farm.",a:["barn"],x:"‘The day starts at half past nine in our old barn’ – visitors shouldn't go to the farmhouse."},{s:"The most popular cheese contains {g}.",a:["garlic"],x:"‘The one people love most is a soft cheese with garlic.’"},{s:"The hard cheese is made with {g}.",a:["apple","apples"],x:"‘a hard cheese which is made with apple’"},{s:"Lunch is homemade soup and {g}.",a:["bread"],x:"‘it's homemade soup and bread, with our cheese of course’"},{s:"Visitors must keep their cheese in the fridge for {g} before eating it.",a:["two weeks","2 weeks"],x:"‘You'll need to keep it in the fridge for two weeks before you eat it.’"},{s:"Visitors should wear old shoes or {g}.",a:["boots"],x:"‘wear old shoes or boots, because the farmyard can be very muddy’"}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a girl called Kira Banks, who plays the drums in a band.",script:[["M","Today I'm talking to Kira Banks, who is seventeen and plays the drums in a band called The Paper Boats. Kira, welcome."],["F","Thanks for having me."],["M","How did you start playing the drums?"],["F","Well, my parents both play the piano, and they wanted me to learn it too. I had lessons for two years, but I never really enjoyed them. Then at a friend's party there was a drum kit in the garage, and I sat down and tried it. It just felt right straight away. I asked for my own drums for my next birthday."],["M","Your neighbours must love you!"],["F","Ha! Actually, that was a big problem at first. We live in a flat, so I can't practise at home. Now I practise at a music studio near my school. It costs a bit, so I work in a shoe shop on Saturdays to pay for it."],["M","How did the band start?"],["F","The guitarist, Josh, put a message on the noticeboard at school. He was looking for a drummer. I was really shy then and almost didn't reply, but my best friend made me do it. We've been together for three years now."],["M","What's the best thing about being in a band?"],["F","People think it's being on stage, and that is great. But for me it's writing songs together. Someone brings a small idea, and two hours later we've got a whole song. That feeling is amazing."],["M","Have you had any difficult moments?"],["F","Our first concert was a disaster. One of my sticks broke in the middle of the second song, and I didn't have another one. I had to play the rest of the song with one stick and my hand! Now I always carry four spare sticks."],["M","What are your plans for the future?"],["F","Lots of people say I should try to become famous, but that's not really my dream. I'd love to study music at university and then teach drums to young people, especially girls, because there still aren't many female drummers."],["M","Kira Banks, thank you."]],qs:[{q:"Why did Kira start playing the drums?",o:["Her parents wanted her to.","She had lessons at school.","She enjoyed trying them at a party."],a:2,x:"‘At a friend's party there was a drum kit… It just felt right straight away.’ Her parents wanted her to play the piano."},{q:"Where does Kira practise?",o:["at a studio","at home","at a friend's house"],a:0,x:"‘I can't practise at home. Now I practise at a music studio near my school.’"},{q:"How did Kira join the band?",o:["Josh heard her play and asked her.","She answered a message at school.","Her best friend was already in the band."],a:1,x:"‘Josh put a message on the noticeboard at school… my best friend made me [reply].’"},{q:"What does Kira enjoy most about being in the band?",o:["performing in front of people","creating songs with the others","travelling to different places"],a:1,x:"‘People think it's being on stage… But for me it's writing songs together.’"},{q:"What went wrong at the band's first concert?",o:["Kira forgot how to play a song.","The audience was very small.","Some of Kira's equipment broke."],a:2,x:"‘One of my sticks broke in the middle of the second song.’"},{q:"What does Kira want to do in the future?",o:["teach young people","become famous","play in a bigger band"],a:0,x:"‘I'd love to… teach drums to young people.’ Becoming famous is ‘not really my dream’."}]}}}),LZ.data.lpapers.b1.push({id:"b1pl2",practice:!0,title:"Practice set 2",topics:"Everyday situations · A pottery workshop · Opinions · A young show jumper",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You will hear a woman buying a train ticket.",q:"Which ticket does she buy?",o:["a single","a return","a weekly ticket"],a:1,x:"‘A return is only two pounds more… Then yes, I'll be back this evening.’ She asked for a single first, and she's only going once, so not a weekly ticket.",script:[["F","Hello, a single to Bristol, please."],["M","Are you coming back today? A return is only two pounds more than a single."],["F","Oh, really? Then yes, I'll be back this evening. I did think about a weekly ticket, but I'm only going once."],["M","That's sixteen pounds fifty, then."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a boy talking to his mother about his homework.",q:"Which subject does he need help with?",o:["science","history","maths"],a:0,x:"‘It's this science question about plants – I don't understand what it's asking.’ He has done the maths, and the history is just reading.",script:[["M","Mum, can you help me with my homework?"],["F","If it's maths, ask your dad – he's much better than me."],["M","No, I've done the maths. And the history is just reading. It's this science question about plants – I don't understand what it's asking."],["F","OK, let me see."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a man talking about a present he bought.",q:"What did he buy for his sister?",o:["a scarf","a watch","a bag"],a:2,x:"‘In the end I found a lovely leather bag in the market.’ The watches were too expensive, and she already has lots of scarves.",script:[["M","I wanted to get my sister a watch, because hers is broken, but they were all too expensive. I almost bought her a scarf, but she's got about twenty already. In the end I found a lovely leather bag in the market – exactly the kind she likes – and it was half price."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a girl leaving a message for a friend.",q:"Where will they meet?",o:["outside the cinema","at the bus station","in a café"],a:2,x:"‘Let's meet in the café opposite instead.’ Outside the cinema was the old plan, and she's coming from the bus station.",script:[["F","Hi Ben, it's Amy. About tonight – I know we said we'd meet outside the cinema, but it's going to rain. Let's meet in the café opposite instead – we can have a hot chocolate before the film. I'll come straight from the bus station. See you at six!"]]},{ctx:"You will hear a man asking for directions.",q:"Where is the post office now?",o:["next to the bank","opposite the library","behind the supermarket"],a:1,x:"‘It's on the left, opposite the library.’ It used to be next to the bank, and you walk past the supermarket.",script:[["M","Excuse me, is the post office near here? I thought it was next to the bank."],["F","It used to be, but it moved last year. Go straight down this road, past the supermarket, and it's on the left, opposite the library."],["M","Thanks very much."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a woman explaining why she is late for work.",q:"Why was she late?",o:["She missed her train.","Her car wouldn't start.","There was a lot of traffic."],a:0,x:"‘I watched my train leave without me.’ The traffic was fine and the car started ‘without any problems’.",script:[["F","Sorry I'm late. The traffic was fine for once, and the car started without any problems. But I stopped to buy a coffee near the station, and the queue was so long that I watched my train leave without me. I had to wait twenty minutes for the next one."]]},{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about their plans for Saturday.",q:"What will they do on Saturday?",o:["go to the beach","go shopping","go for a bike ride"],a:2,x:"‘Let's go for a bike ride along the river.’ – ‘OK, good idea.’ The beach will be cold and he has no money for shopping.",script:[["M","What shall we do on Saturday? The beach?"],["F","It's going to be cloudy and cold. What about shopping in town?"],["M","I spent all my money last weekend. Let's go for a bike ride along the river – it's free!"],["F","OK, good idea. I'll bring some sandwiches."]]}]},2:{extracts:[{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a concert.",q:"What did the girl think of the concert?",o:["It was too short.","The sound was poor.","The tickets cost too much."],a:0,x:"‘They only played for forty minutes! I couldn't believe it.’ The sound was ‘perfect’ and the tickets ‘weren't bad’.",script:[["M","How was the concert last night?"],["F","The band were great, and the sound was perfect, even at the back. But they only played for forty minutes! I couldn't believe it. They didn't even do my favourite song."],["M","For the price of those tickets? That's terrible."],["F","Well, the tickets weren't bad, actually."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a man telling a friend about learning to drive.",q:"How does he feel now?",o:["embarrassed about failing his test","nervous about driving with passengers","proud of what he has done"],a:2,x:"‘I passed last week… I'm really pleased with myself.’ He was embarrassed after the first test, and he's only nervous on the motorway.",script:[["F","How are the driving lessons going?"],["M","I failed my test the first time, and I was so embarrassed. But I passed last week! I still get a bit nervous on the motorway, but yesterday I drove my mum to the airport all by myself. I'm really pleased with myself."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a girl talking to her father about a new teacher.",q:"What does she like about the new teacher?",o:["She gives very little homework.","She explains things clearly.","She is very funny."],a:1,x:"‘When she explains something, I actually understand it the first time.’ She gives more homework and is ‘quite serious’.",script:[["M","How's your new maths teacher?"],["F","Well, she gives us more homework than Mr Patel did, and she's quite serious. But when she explains something, I actually understand it the first time. She draws pictures on the board and uses examples from real life."],["M","That's what matters."]]},{ctx:"You will hear two friends talking about a café.",q:"What do they disagree about?",o:["the food","the service","the prices"],a:2,x:"She says it's ‘good value’, he thinks it's ‘really expensive’. They agree the cakes are delicious and the staff are friendly.",script:[["F","I love this café. The cakes are the best in town."],["M","They're delicious – I agree. And the staff are always friendly."],["F","And it's good value too."],["M","Good value? Four pounds for a small coffee? I think it's really expensive."],["F","Not compared to the places near the station."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a boy telling a friend about his summer job.",q:"Why did he choose this job?",o:["He wanted to work outside.","It pays very well.","His friend works there too."],a:0,x:"‘I really wanted a job where I could be in the fresh air.’ The money ‘isn't great’ and none of his friends work there.",script:[["F","I heard you're working at the garden centre this summer."],["M","Yes. The money isn't great, to be honest, and none of my friends work there. But I spent all year in classrooms, and I really wanted a job where I could be in the fresh air. I'm watering plants and carrying trees all day – I love it."]]},{ctx:"You will hear a woman telling a friend about a book club.",q:"Why did she join it?",o:["to read more books","to meet new people","because her sister asked her to"],a:1,x:"‘I moved here and I didn't know anyone. This club seemed a good way to make some friends.’ She already read a lot, and she didn't join her sister's club.",script:[["M","Since when are you in a book club?"],["F","Since March. I've always read a lot, so that's not why. My sister's been in one for years and kept telling me to join hers, but I didn't want to. Then I moved here and I didn't know anyone. This club seemed a good way to make some friends – and it worked."]]}]},3:{title:"Pottery Workshop",ctx:"You will hear a woman called Helen Moss talking on the radio about a pottery workshop for beginners.",script:[["F",`Hi, I'm Helen Moss, and I run a pottery studio in Hartley, right next to the railway station. Every month I hold a one-day workshop for complete beginners, and I'd like to tell you about the next one.

It's on Saturday the ninth of November, from ten in the morning until four in the afternoon. There are only eight places, because I like to give everyone plenty of help.

In the morning, you'll learn to make a bowl by hand. In the afternoon, you'll try the potter's wheel, which most people find the hardest part – but also the most fun. Don't worry if your first pot falls over. Everybody's does!

After the workshop, I'll put your pots in a very hot oven to make them hard. They'll be ready to collect after ten days. The price is fifty-five pounds, and that includes all materials and a light lunch. Please wear clothes you don't mind getting dirty, and if you have long hair, bring something to tie it back.

You can book by phone or at the studio. The studio is closed on Mondays.`]],items:[{s:"The studio is next to the {g}.",a:["railway station","station","train station"],x:"‘I run a pottery studio in Hartley, right next to the railway station.’"},{s:"Date of the next workshop: Saturday {g}",a:["9 November","9th November","9th of November","the 9th of November","ninth of November","the ninth of November","November 9","November 9th","9/11","9.11"],x:"‘It's on Saturday the ninth of November.’"},{s:"In the morning, people make a {g} by hand.",a:["bowl"],x:"‘In the morning, you'll learn to make a bowl by hand.’ The wheel is in the afternoon."},{s:"The pots can be collected after {g}.",a:["ten days","10 days"],x:"‘They'll be ready to collect after ten days.’"},{s:"The price includes all materials and a {g}.",a:["light lunch","lunch"],x:"‘that includes all materials and a light lunch’"},{s:"People with long hair should bring something to {g} it back.",a:["tie"],x:"‘if you have long hair, bring something to tie it back’"}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a boy called Owen Price, who has won a show-jumping competition for young riders.",script:[["F","My guest today is sixteen-year-old Owen Price, who has just won a national show-jumping competition for young riders. Congratulations, Owen."],["M","Thank you."],["F","How did you get interested in horses?"],["M","Nobody in my family rides. But when I was seven, we went on holiday to a farm, and the farmer let me sit on his old horse, Duke. I didn't want to get off! When we got home, I asked for riding lessons every day until my parents said yes."],["F","Do you have your own horse?"],["M","Not exactly. My horse, Pepper, belongs to a woman who lives near us. She's too busy to ride her, so I look after her and ride her in competitions. I pay for some of Pepper's food with money from my weekend job at the stables."],["F","How much do you train?"],["M","I ride every day before school, from six until half past seven. People think jumping is the main thing, but most of the training is on the ground – teaching Pepper to listen to me and understand what I want. The jumps are only a small part of it."],["F","Tell me about the competition you won."],["M","Honestly, I didn't expect to win. The week before, Pepper had a problem with her leg, and I nearly didn't go. On the day she was fine, but I was so nervous that I couldn't eat any breakfast. When I saw our time at the end, I just started laughing."],["F","What's the hardest thing about the sport?"],["M","Falling off isn't the worst thing – it happens to everyone. It's the cost. Competitions, transport, equipment – it all adds up. Some very good young riders have to stop because their families can't pay."],["F","And what are your plans?"],["M","I'd love to ride for my country one day. But first I want to finish school and study to become a vet. That way, I'll always be working with horses, even if I don't become a top rider."],["F","Owen Price, thank you."]],qs:[{q:"How did Owen become interested in riding?",o:["His family has always had horses.","He sat on a horse on a farm holiday.","He saw a competition on TV."],a:1,x:"‘We went on holiday to a farm, and the farmer let me sit on his old horse.’ ‘Nobody in my family rides.’"},{q:"What does Owen say about Pepper?",o:["She belongs to someone else.","He bought her with his own money.","She used to belong to the farmer."],a:0,x:"‘Pepper belongs to a woman who lives near us.’ His money only pays for some of her food."},{q:"What does Owen say about his training?",o:["He trains after school.","Most of it involves jumping.","Most of it is about communicating with his horse."],a:2,x:"‘Most of the training is on the ground – teaching Pepper to listen to me.’ He trains before school."},{q:"How did Owen feel on the day of the competition?",o:["sure that he would win","very nervous","worried about Pepper's leg"],a:1,x:"‘I was so nervous that I couldn't eat any breakfast.’ Pepper's leg was a problem the week before, and on the day she was fine."},{q:"What does Owen find hardest about show jumping?",o:["how expensive it is","how often riders fall","how much time it takes"],a:0,x:"‘Falling off isn't the worst thing… It's the cost.’"},{q:"What does Owen plan to do next?",o:["ride for his country","continue his education","work at the stables full-time"],a:1,x:"‘First I want to finish school and study to become a vet.’ Riding for his country is a dream for ‘one day’."}]}}}),LZ.data.lpapers=LZ.data.lpapers||{b1:[],c1:[]},(LZ.data.lpapers.c1=LZ.data.lpapers.c1||[]).push({id:"c1pl1",practice:!0,title:"Practice set 1",topics:"Three conversations · A falconer · A conflict mediator · Giving up a hobby",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You hear two friends talking about the final of a television baking competition.",script:[["F","Did you see the final of that baking competition last night?"],["M","I did. I have to admit, I only started watching because everyone at work kept talking about it, but I'm completely hooked now."],["F","Me too. Though I thought the judges got it wrong in the end. Sam's cake was technically perfect, I'll give them that, but Lucy took a real risk with those flavours, and surely that's what a final should reward."],["M","I'm not so sure. A risk that doesn't quite come off is still a mistake. What bothered me more was how much of the episode went on the contestants' life stories. I wanted to watch them bake."],["F","Oh, I like that part. It's what makes you care who wins."],["M","Maybe, but it felt like padding to me."]],qs:[{q:"What does the woman think about the result of the final?",o:["The judges should have valued originality more highly.","The winning cake was not technically good enough.","The two finalists were too evenly matched to judge."],a:0,x:"‘Lucy took a real risk with those flavours, and surely that's what a final should reward.’ She accepts that Sam's cake ‘was technically perfect’."},{q:"What did the man dislike about the episode?",o:["the judges' comments on the flavours","the time given to the contestants' backgrounds","the difficulty of the final challenge"],a:1,x:"‘What bothered me more was how much of the episode went on the contestants' life stories… it felt like padding.’"}]},{ctx:"You hear two students talking about a group project.",script:[["M","How's your group getting on with the history presentation?"],["F","Better now, thankfully. For the first couple of weeks it was chaos – four of us researching exactly the same things and nobody actually writing anything. In the end I just drew up a list of who'd do what and sent it round. I felt a bit bossy, to be honest, but nobody objected. I think they were relieved."],["M","We've got the opposite problem. Everyone's done their bit, but separately, so nothing joins up. It's like four different presentations stuck end to end."],["F","Can't one of you go through it and smooth it out?"],["M","That's the idea. Though I suspect it'll end up being me, as usual."]],qs:[{q:"How did the woman feel about what she did in her group?",o:["She resented having to do more than the others.","She was uneasy about taking charge.","She was proud of the research she had done."],a:1,x:"She drew up a list of tasks: ‘I felt a bit bossy, to be honest.’"},{q:"What is the problem with the man's group's presentation?",o:["The separate sections don't fit together.","Some members have not done their share.","It is much too long."],a:0,x:"‘Everyone's done their bit, but separately, so nothing joins up.’"}]},{ctx:"You hear two neighbours talking about a plan to build a wind farm near their village.",script:[["F","Have you been to one of the meetings about the wind farm yet?"],["M","Last Tuesday. I went expecting to be against it, to be honest – I'd heard all sorts about the noise. But the engineer they'd brought along was very straightforward. He played us recordings from a similar site, and you could barely hear the turbines over the birds. I came away thinking my worries had been overblown."],["F","The noise was never my concern. It's the road. They'll need to bring those enormous blades in somehow, and our lane isn't built for that."],["M","They said it would only be for a few months, while it's being built."],["F","That's what they always say. And a few months of lorries going past the school is a few months too many."]],qs:[{q:"What changed the man's opinion about the wind farm?",o:["the support of other people in the village","evidence presented at a meeting","a visit to another wind farm"],a:1,x:"The engineer ‘played us recordings from a similar site, and you could barely hear the turbines’. He didn't visit the site himself."},{q:"What is the woman worried about?",o:["the effect on local wildlife","noise during the building work","heavy traffic near the school"],a:2,x:"‘The noise was never my concern… a few months of lorries going past the school is a few months too many.’"}]}]},2:{ctx:"You will hear a woman called Rosie Hart talking about her work as a falconer.",script:[["F",`Hello, everyone. I'm Rosie Hart, and for the past fifteen years I've worked with birds of prey. Most people assume falconry is a hobby for the wealthy, or something you see at a medieval fair, but these days most of my income actually comes from pest control. I'm hired by hospitals, shopping centres and even a football stadium to keep pigeons and gulls away – not by catching them, but simply by flying a hawk where they can see it. The other birds soon decide to go somewhere else.

I got started in an unusual way. I was working as a vet's receptionist, and one day someone brought in an injured owl. The vet asked me to look after it over a weekend, and by Monday morning I'd decided this was what I wanted to do with my life.

People often ask which bird is the most difficult to train. The answer surprises them: it isn't the big eagles, which are actually quite calm, but the little kestrels. They're nervous and easily distracted, and you need enormous patience.

Training is based entirely on food. A hawk doesn't do anything out of affection; it comes back to the glove because it has learned that's where its next meal is. The key is weight. I weigh every bird every morning, because even twenty grams can make the difference between a bird that's keen to work and one that simply flies off and sits in a tree.

That has happened to me more often than I'd like to admit. The longest I've ever had to wait for a bird to come back was nine hours – I stood in a farmer's field until it was completely dark, holding up a piece of chicken.

These days I also visit schools. The children are usually most excited by the owls, but what I want them to come away with is respect, not just excitement. These are wild animals, not pets.

If any of you are thinking of taking it up, my advice is to start by volunteering at a bird sanctuary. You'll learn more in a month of cleaning out aviaries than you will from any book.`]],items:[{s:"Most of Rosie's income now comes from {g}.",a:["pest control"],x:"‘these days most of my income actually comes from pest control’ – not from fairs or wealthy hobbyists."},{s:"Rosie first became interested in birds of prey while she was working as a {g}.",a:["vet's receptionist","vet receptionist","receptionist"],x:"‘I was working as a vet's receptionist, and one day someone brought in an injured owl.’"},{s:"Rosie says the most difficult bird to train is the {g}.",a:["kestrel","kestrels"],x:"‘it isn't the big eagles… but the little kestrels’."},{s:"Every morning, Rosie checks each bird's {g}.",a:["weight"],x:"‘The key is weight. I weigh every bird every morning.’"},{s:"The longest Rosie has had to wait for a bird to return is {g}.",a:["nine hours","9 hours"],x:"‘The longest I've ever had to wait for a bird to come back was nine hours.’"},{s:"While she waited, Rosie stood in a {g} until it got dark.",a:["farmer's field","field"],x:"‘I stood in a farmer's field until it was completely dark.’"},{s:"Rosie wants schoolchildren to leave with a feeling of {g} for the birds.",a:["respect"],x:"‘what I want them to come away with is respect, not just excitement.’"},{s:"Rosie suggests that beginners start by volunteering at a {g}.",a:["bird sanctuary","sanctuary"],x:"‘start by volunteering at a bird sanctuary’."}]},3:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a mediator called Daniel Fry, who helps people settle disputes.",script:[["F","My guest today is Daniel Fry, who works as a mediator, helping people settle disagreements without going to court. Daniel, how did you end up doing this?"],["M","Rather by accident. I trained as a lawyer, and I was good at winning arguments. But I noticed that even when my clients won, they often left court angrier than when they'd arrived. Winning wasn't the same as resolving anything. When a colleague mentioned a mediation course, I signed up mainly out of curiosity – and I never went back."],["F","What kinds of dispute do you deal with?"],["M","Everything from neighbours arguing over a fence to business partners splitting up. People imagine the big commercial cases are the hardest, but they're often the most straightforward, because there's money involved and money can be divided. The really difficult ones are between neighbours. They have to go on living next to each other afterwards, and the dispute is hardly ever about the fence. It's about feeling disrespected."],["F","So what actually happens in a mediation session?"],["M","The most important thing I do is very simple: I make sure each person feels heard. Often I'll ask one party to repeat back what the other has said, in their own words, before they're allowed to respond. People find that surprisingly hard. But once someone feels genuinely understood, they become far more willing to compromise."],["F","Do you ever take sides?"],["M","Never openly. Privately, of course, I sometimes think one person is being more reasonable than the other. But the moment either of them suspects I've made up my mind, I've lost them. My job isn't to decide who's right – it's to help them find an agreement they can both live with."],["F","Are there cases that can't be mediated?"],["M","Some. If one person is frightened of the other, mediation isn't appropriate – they need protection, not negotiation. And occasionally you meet someone who doesn't really want a solution; the conflict has become part of who they are. You can usually tell within the first ten minutes."],["F","And what has the job taught you personally?"],["M","To argue less at home, my wife would say! Seriously, I've learned to stop trying to win every disagreement. Most of the time, being right matters a great deal less than we think."]],qs:[{q:"Why did Daniel give up working as a lawyer?",o:["He was not successful in court.","He realised that winning a case did not end the conflict.","A colleague persuaded him that mediation was better paid.","He was tired of working long hours."],a:1,x:"‘even when my clients won, they often left court angrier… Winning wasn't the same as resolving anything.’"},{q:"According to Daniel, why are disputes between neighbours especially difficult?",o:["They usually involve large sums of money.","The legal questions are often complicated.","The real cause is usually hurt feelings rather than the stated problem.","Neighbours are often unwilling to attend the sessions."],a:2,x:"‘the dispute is hardly ever about the fence. It's about feeling disrespected.’ Money cases are ‘the most straightforward’."},{q:"What does Daniel consider the most important part of his role?",o:["making sure both people feel listened to","explaining the law to each party","proposing a fair compromise","keeping the sessions as short as possible"],a:0,x:"‘The most important thing I do is very simple: I make sure each person feels heard.’"},{q:"What does Daniel say about taking sides?",o:["He sometimes tells the more reasonable person privately.","It is acceptable when one person is clearly in the wrong.","He has his own opinions but must not let them show.","He never forms a view about who is right."],a:2,x:"‘Privately… I sometimes think one person is being more reasonable… But the moment either of them suspects I've made up my mind, I've lost them.’"},{q:"When does Daniel believe mediation is unsuitable?",o:["when the dispute involves a business","when one person is afraid of the other","when the people involved have tried it before","when the disagreement has gone on for years"],a:1,x:"‘If one person is frightened of the other, mediation isn't appropriate.’"},{q:"What has Daniel learned from his work?",o:["that winning arguments is less important than he once thought","that he should have become a mediator much earlier","that his wife is a better negotiator than he is","that most disagreements can never be fully resolved"],a:0,x:"‘I've learned to stop trying to win every disagreement… being right matters a great deal less than we think.’"}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear five short extracts in which people talk about giving up a hobby they had done for many years.",tasks:[{prompt:"For questions 21–25, choose from the list (A–H) why each speaker gave up their hobby.",options:["it had become too expensive","an injury","a lack of time because of work","moving to a new area","losing interest after reaching a goal","a change in the rules","the hobby had begun to feel like a duty","a disagreement with others involved"]},{prompt:"For questions 26–30, choose from the list (A–H) how each speaker feels about it now.",options:["relieved to have more free time","keen to take it up again one day","regretful about the decision","surprised by how little they miss it","pleased to have found something new","grateful for the friendships it gave them","proud of what they achieved","worried about losing their fitness"]}],speakers:[{a:[1,3],x:["B: ‘I damaged my shoulder… the doctor said rowing was out.’ People assumed it was the higher fees, but it wasn't.","D: ‘I'd assumed I couldn't live without it. But a year on, I hardly think about it.’"],script:[["M","I rowed for nearly twenty years – early mornings on the river, races most weekends. People assumed I stopped because the club had just put its fees up, but it was my shoulder. I damaged it lifting a sofa, of all things, and the doctor said rowing was out for at least a year. I was devastated at the time; I'd assumed I couldn't live without it. But a year on, I hardly think about it. I walk along the river and watch the boats go past and feel – nothing much, really. Which says something, I suppose."]]},{a:[7,5],x:["H: the new conductor wanted to compete; ‘A few of us argued with him about it… in the end it was easier to leave.’","F: ‘Some of my closest friends are people I met in that choir.’"],script:[["F","I sang in a choir for twelve years and loved every minute. But when a new conductor arrived, things changed. He wanted us to enter competitions, and rehearsals became terribly serious. A few of us argued with him about it, quite openly, and in the end it was easier to leave. What I'm left with, though, isn't bitterness. Some of my closest friends are people I met in that choir, and I'll always be thankful for that. We still meet every week – we just don't sing."]]},{a:[2,1],x:["C: ‘I was promoted, and suddenly I was travelling for work every other week.’","B: ‘The moment things calm down at work, I'll be back.’"],script:[["M","I'd been doing amateur theatre since I was a teenager – two or three productions a year. Then I was promoted, and suddenly I was travelling for work every other week. You can't learn your lines in a hotel room at midnight – well, you can, but you'll have forgotten them by the time you're on stage. So I stepped down, telling myself it was only temporary. And it is. The moment things calm down at work, I'll be back. I've already asked the director to keep a part for me."]]},{a:[6,0],x:["G: ‘it stopped being fun… as though I'd signed up for extra work.’ She wasn't injured or busy.","A: ‘having my weekends back is wonderful. I read, I sleep late, I see friends.’"],script:[["F","I played in a tennis league for years. At some point – I couldn't tell you exactly when – it stopped being fun. I'd look at the fixtures list on a Sunday evening and feel a kind of dread, as though I'd signed up for extra work. I wasn't injured, I wasn't busy; I just didn't want to go any more. Giving it up was easy. And honestly, having my weekends back is wonderful. I read, I sleep late, I see friends. I should have stopped years earlier."]]},{a:[4,4],x:["E: after climbing the last summit, ‘the urge had just gone’.","E: ‘Since then I've got into sea kayaking… and I love it.’"],script:[["M","For most of my thirties, my goal was to climb all the highest peaks in Scotland. It took eight years, and the day I reached the last summit, I sat on a rock and thought, well, that's that. I'd assumed I'd keep going – find another challenge – but the urge had just gone. Since then I've got into sea kayaking, which I never imagined doing, and I love it. Different landscape, same sense of freedom."]]}]}}},{id:"c1pl2",practice:!0,title:"Practice set 2",topics:"Three conversations · Restoring stained glass · A behavioural economist · Running a market stall",parts:{1:{extracts:[{ctx:"You hear two friends talking about buying tickets for a music festival.",script:[["M","Did you manage to get tickets for the festival in the end?"],["F","Eventually. The website crashed twice, and when I finally got through, the cheaper tickets had all gone. I paid nearly double what I'd planned."],["M","That's outrageous. You should complain."],["F","I thought about it, but honestly, it's my own fault. I knew they'd sell out fast, and I could have signed up for the early sale months ago. I just kept putting it off."],["M","Well, I'm not even trying this year. Half the fun of those things is going with a crowd of friends, and everyone I know has either moved away or has small children now."],["F","That's a shame. You could always come with us."]],qs:[{q:"How does the woman feel about the price she paid?",o:["She blames herself for not acting sooner.","She intends to ask for some of the money back.","She thinks the festival is worth the extra cost."],a:0,x:"‘it's my own fault… I could have signed up for the early sale months ago. I just kept putting it off.’"},{q:"Why isn't the man going to the festival this year?",o:["He cannot afford the tickets.","He has nobody to go with.","He didn't enjoy it last time."],a:1,x:"‘Half the fun… is going with a crowd of friends, and everyone I know has either moved away or has small children now.’"}]},{ctx:"You hear two colleagues talking about a training day they attended.",script:[["F","What did you make of yesterday's training day?"],["M","The morning was a waste of time, frankly. Three hours of slides telling us things we've all known for years."],["F","I thought the trainer was quite entertaining, though."],["M","Entertaining, yes. Useful, no. The afternoon was a different story. Actually working through those problems with people from other departments – I learned more in an hour than in the rest of the day put together. I'd never really understood what the finance team does."],["F","Same here. I'd like to see much more of that, rather than sitting through a lecture."],["M","Shall we suggest it? They did ask for feedback."]],qs:[{q:"What was the man's opinion of the morning session?",o:["The trainer was poorly prepared.","The content was too complicated.","It told them nothing new."],a:2,x:"‘Three hours of slides telling us things we've all known for years.’"},{q:"What do they agree about the afternoon session?",o:["Working with other departments was valuable.","It should have lasted longer.","The problems they worked on were unrealistic."],a:0,x:"He ‘learned more in an hour’ working with other departments; she says ‘Same here… I'd like to see much more of that.’"}]},{ctx:"You hear two friends talking about learning to drive as adults.",script:[["M","So how are the driving lessons going?"],["F","Slowly. I'd assumed that because I'm forty and reasonably sensible, I'd pick it up quickly. But my instructor says I think too much. I check the mirrors so often that I forget to look at the road."],["M","Ha! I was the opposite when I learned. Far too confident. I failed my first test for going round a roundabout as if I owned it."],["F","Well, at least you didn't have a teenager in the back seat giving you advice. My son's just passed his test, and he's unbearable."],["M","Does he actually help, though?"],["F","Annoyingly, yes. He's a very good explainer. But I'll never tell him that."]],qs:[{q:"What does the woman say about her driving lessons?",o:["She is making faster progress than she expected.","She is too cautious.","She finds her instructor unhelpful."],a:1,x:"‘I check the mirrors so often that I forget to look at the road.’ Her progress is slow."},{q:"How does the woman feel about her son's advice?",o:["She finds it useful but won't admit it to him.","She thinks it is out of date.","She would rather he kept it to himself."],a:0,x:"‘Annoyingly, yes. He's a very good explainer. But I'll never tell him that.’"}]}]},2:{ctx:"You will hear a man called Owen Price talking about his work restoring stained-glass windows.",script:[["M",`Good afternoon. I'm Owen Price, and I restore stained-glass windows – mostly in churches, but also in old houses, pubs and, once, a railway station. People are often surprised to hear that the biggest threat to old glass isn't vandalism or storms. It's the lead. The strips of lead that hold the pieces together only last about a hundred and twenty years, and after that the whole window starts to sag and bulge like a sail.

I came into the trade from a slightly odd direction – I was originally a jewellery maker. The skills are closer than you might think: both involve working with very small, fragile pieces and joining them together accurately.

When a window comes into the workshop, the first thing we do is photograph it and make a rubbing – a full-size pencil tracing of every line of lead. That rubbing becomes our map. Without it, putting several hundred pieces back in the right place would be almost impossible.

Cleaning is the most delicate stage. Many windows have painted details – faces, hands, lettering – and after centuries some of that paint is barely attached. We use cotton buds and distilled water, and sometimes nothing more than a soft brush. You'd be amazed how often cleaning reveals colours nobody knew were there.

Where a piece is missing, we match the original glass as closely as we can, but we always mark the new piece with the year, scratched into the edge where nobody will see it. Future restorers need to know what's original and what isn't.

The most memorable window I've worked on was in a small village church. When we took it apart, we found a note tucked behind the lead, written by the glazier who'd made it in 1880. All it said was that it had rained every day that week.

A typical church window takes us around three months. And the question I'm asked most often is how much it costs. My honest answer is: less than replacing it – and nothing like as much as losing it.`]],items:[{s:"Owen says the biggest threat to old windows comes from the {g}.",a:["lead"],x:"‘the biggest threat to old glass isn't vandalism or storms. It's the lead.’"},{s:"After about 120 years, a window may start to bulge like a {g}.",a:["sail"],x:"‘the whole window starts to sag and bulge like a sail.’"},{s:"Before becoming a restorer, Owen worked as a {g}.",a:["jewellery maker","jewelry maker","jeweller","jeweler"],x:"‘I was originally a jewellery maker.’"},{s:"Owen describes the full-size {g} of a window as the team's map.",a:["rubbing","pencil tracing","tracing"],x:"‘make a rubbing – a full-size pencil tracing… That rubbing becomes our map.’"},{s:"For cleaning, Owen's team uses cotton buds and {g}.",a:["distilled water"],x:"‘We use cotton buds and distilled water.’"},{s:"Each new piece of glass is marked with the {g}.",a:["year"],x:"‘we always mark the new piece with the year, scratched into the edge’."},{s:"A note found in one window said that it had {g} every day that week.",a:["rained"],x:"‘All it said was that it had rained every day that week.’"},{s:"Restoring a typical church window takes about {g}.",a:["three months","3 months"],x:"‘A typical church window takes us around three months.’"}]},3:{ctx:"You will hear an interview with a behavioural economist called Dr Sarah Lin, who studies how people make decisions about money.",script:[["M","My guest today is Dr Sarah Lin, a behavioural economist. Sarah, what exactly does a behavioural economist do?"],["F","Traditional economics assumed that people make rational decisions – that we weigh up costs and benefits carefully and choose whatever leaves us better off. Behavioural economists study what people actually do, which is often rather different. I got into it as a student, when I realised that the elegant models in my textbooks couldn't explain why my flatmates kept running out of money by the middle of every month."],["M","Can you give us an example of irrational behaviour?"],["F","One of my favourites is prices ending in nine. Logically, there's almost no difference between nine ninety-nine and ten pounds. But we read from left to right, and the first digit makes a disproportionate impression. In our experiments, simply changing a price from ten pounds to nine ninety-nine increased sales by around a fifth – far more than a penny could possibly justify."],["M","Do shops exploit these tendencies?"],["F","Of course, though I'd be careful with the word ‘exploit’. A lot of what shops do is simply responding to how customers behave. Where I do get concerned is with things like subscriptions that are easy to start and deliberately difficult to cancel. Companies know perfectly well that many people will forget, and they're relying on it. That's where I think regulators should step in."],["M","Can understanding these biases help people make better decisions?"],["F","Less than you'd hope, unfortunately. Knowing about a bias doesn't make you immune to it – I fall for the nine-ninety-nine trick myself. What works much better is changing the situation rather than the person. If your employer automatically puts part of your salary into savings unless you opt out, you'll almost certainly save more than if you have to choose to do it."],["M","Isn't that a little manipulative?"],["F","It's a fair question, and one we debate a lot. My view is that there's no such thing as a neutral choice. Every form has a default option, whether anyone designed it deliberately or not. So the question isn't whether to influence people, but whether we do it in their interests."],["M","And what are you working on now?"],["F","How people make decisions when they're tired. It turns out we choose very differently at the end of a long day – we go for whatever's easiest. Which explains a great deal about what ends up in my own shopping basket on a Friday evening."]],qs:[{q:"What first made Sarah interested in behavioural economics?",o:["a lecture by a well-known economist","noticing how people she lived with handled money","her own difficulties in managing money","a research project on student spending"],a:1,x:"The textbook models ‘couldn't explain why my flatmates kept running out of money’."},{q:"What point does Sarah make about prices ending in nine?",o:["Most shoppers can see through the trick.","The effect is smaller than many people believe.","They influence sales far more than seems reasonable.","They work better for cheap goods than for expensive ones."],a:2,x:"Sales rose ‘by around a fifth – far more than a penny could possibly justify’."},{q:"What is Sarah's view of the way companies behave?",o:["Most shops deliberately deceive their customers.","Supermarkets are more honest than online businesses.","Customers should take more responsibility for their choices.","Some business practices need to be controlled."],a:3,x:"She is careful with ‘exploit’, but subscriptions that are hard to cancel are ‘where I think regulators should step in’."},{q:"According to Sarah, what is the most effective way to improve people's decisions?",o:["teaching them about common biases","giving them more information about prices","changing the circumstances in which they choose","rewarding them for saving money"],a:2,x:"‘Knowing about a bias doesn't make you immune to it… What works much better is changing the situation rather than the person.’"},{q:"How does Sarah respond to the suggestion that this approach is manipulative?",o:["She admits that it is wrong in some cases.","She argues that every choice is influenced in some way.","She says people can always opt out if they wish.","She thinks the question is not worth discussing."],a:1,x:"‘there's no such thing as a neutral choice. Every form has a default option.’ She calls it ‘a fair question’."},{q:"What does Sarah suggest about her current research?",o:["It helps to explain some of her own behaviour.","It has produced surprising results about weekends.","It focuses on people who work very long hours.","It has changed the way she does her shopping."],a:0,x:"Tired people go for whatever's easiest, ‘which explains a great deal about what ends up in my own shopping basket on a Friday evening.’"}]},4:{ctx:"You will hear five short extracts in which people talk about running a stall at a local market.",tasks:[{prompt:"For questions 21–25, choose from the list (A–H) why each speaker started running a market stall.",options:["to test a business idea cheaply","to add to a pension","to sell things they had made as a hobby","after losing their job","to clear a relative's belongings","to meet people after moving to the area","to raise money for a charity","because a friend needed a partner"]},{prompt:"For questions 26–30, choose from the list (A–H) what each speaker finds most difficult about it.",options:["working outdoors in bad weather","the early starts","competition from nearby stalls","dealing with rude customers","the physical effort of setting up","keeping track of the money","not knowing how much they will earn","finding time to make new stock"]}],speakers:[{a:[2,7],x:["C: ‘I'd been making pottery in my garden shed for years… people would actually pay for it.’","H: ‘each mug takes me ages… I spend every evening at the wheel trying to keep up.’ The weather doesn't bother her."],script:[["F","I'd been making pottery in my garden shed for years, mostly giving it away to friends, until my sister pointed out that people would actually pay for it. So I booked a pitch at the Saturday market. It's gone far better than I hoped – I often sell out by lunchtime. The trouble is, each mug takes me ages, and I've still got a full-time job. The weather doesn't bother me – I just wrap up warm – but I spend every evening at the wheel trying to keep up, and it's exhausting."]]},{a:[3,6],x:["D: ‘When the factory closed, I was fifty-eight, and nobody seemed keen to employ me.’","G: ‘What I can't get used to is not knowing what I'll take home.’"],script:[["M","When the factory closed, I was fifty-eight, and nobody seemed keen to employ me. A neighbour suggested I try selling second-hand tools at the market, since I know a fair bit about them. I enjoy it – the chat, the regulars. What I can't get used to is not knowing what I'll take home. One week I'll do very nicely, the next I'll barely cover the cost of the pitch. After forty years of a steady wage, that's hard."]]},{a:[0,1],x:["A: ‘A market stall seemed the obvious way to find out without risking much.’","B: ‘The bit I really struggle with is the alarm going off at four.’"],script:[["M","I'd had this idea for a range of hot sauces for ages, but opening a shop would have cost a fortune, and I had no idea whether anyone would buy them. A market stall seemed the obvious way to find out without risking much. It's worked – I'm now talking to a couple of delis about stocking them. The bit I really struggle with is the alarm going off at four. I'm not a morning person, and by the time the customers arrive I've already been up for hours."]]},{a:[4,3],x:["E: ‘When my aunt died, she left a house absolutely full of things… I started taking boxes down to the market.’","D: ‘some will… sneer at the price… That really gets to me.’"],script:[["F","When my aunt died, she left a house absolutely full of things – books, china, old records. We couldn't bear to just throw it all away, so I started taking boxes down to the market on Sundays. It's been oddly comforting, telling people where things came from. Most people are lovely. But some will pick something up, sneer at the price and offer you a fraction of it, as if you're trying to cheat them. That really gets to me, especially when it's her things."]]},{a:[5,4],x:["F: ‘We moved here last year and I didn't know a soul… It's been the best way of getting to know the town.’","E: putting up the gazebo ‘takes nearly an hour, and by the time I've finished I need a sit-down.’"],script:[["F","We moved here last year and I didn't know a soul. I've always baked, so I thought, why not a cake stall? It's been the best way of getting to know the town – half my customers are friends now. Money isn't really the point. What I hadn't bargained for is the gazebo. Putting it up on my own, with weights on each leg and tables and boxes everywhere, takes nearly an hour, and by the time I've finished I need a sit-down."]]}]}}});
